/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES batch 3 (shared Latin/Greek roots) + aliases
   ===================================================== */
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.alias, { bol: "bolo", fer2: "fer", pos2: "pos", ver3: "vert", tract2: "tract", corr: "curr", clud: "clav", meter: "mens", fa: "fat" });
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  act: ["agere(驱赶、做)来自印欧语 *h₂eǵ-(驱赶),同根有希腊语 agein(引导——pedagogue 是\"领孩子的\",demagogue 领民众的,synagogue 是\"把人聚在一起\"的地方)、梵语 ajati(赶牛)、古挪威语 aka(驾车)。罗马人先用它赶牛羊(agere pecus),然后用于一切\"推动\":agere causam 打官司(agent 是替人办事的),actio 行动、也是诉讼(action),actus 演出的一幕(act;actor 先指原告,后指演员),agenda 是\"该做的事\"(1650 年代教会用语),agilis 灵活(agile),ex-igere 逼出——要求(exact 是逼准的,exigent 紧急),cogere 是 co-agere 赶到一起——强迫(cogent 是逼人信服的),ambiguus 是\"两边赶\"——模棱,navigare 驾船,litigare 打官司,agitare 反复驱赶——搅动(agitate)。",
    "agere (to drive, do) comes from PIE *h₂eǵ- (to drive), shared with Greek agein (to lead — a pedagogue leads the child, a demagogue leads the people, a synagogue is where people are led together), Sanskrit ajati (drives cattle), Old Norse aka (to drive a cart). Romans first drove flocks with it (agere pecus), then anything that needed pushing: agere causam to plead a case (an agent acts for another), actio a deed and a lawsuit (action), actus a scene of a play (act; an actor was a plaintiff before a player), agenda \"things to be done\" (Church usage of the 1650s), agilis nimble (agile), ex-igere to drive out — demand (exact is driven to the mark, exigent pressing), cogere is co-agere, to drive together — compel (cogent compels belief), ambiguus \"driven both ways\" — ambiguous, navigare to drive a ship, litigare to drive a suit, agitare to keep driving — agitate."],
  press: ["premere(压)的分词 pressus 是这一族的主干,词源不明,可能是拉丁语独有。罗马人 premere 是压、榨(葡萄、橄榄),也说\"压住敌军\"。ex-primere 是挤出来——表达(express;espresso 是意大利语\"压出来的\"咖啡,1900 年代),im-primere 压印上去(imprint、impression 先是印痕,后指印象;print 是它的法语缩短形 preinte,印刷术 1450 年后借用),com-primere 压紧(compress),de-primere 压下去(depress;经济萧条义 1930 年代固定),op-primere 压迫(oppress),re-primere 压回去(repress),sup-primere 压制(suppress),pressura 压力(pressure),pressa 是压榨机——印刷机 press,再指报刊界(1820 年代)。",
    "The participle pressus of premere (to press) carries the family; the verb is of unknown origin, perhaps purely Latin. Romans premere grapes and olives, and pressed hard on an enemy. ex-primere is pressing out — to express (espresso is Italian for \"pressed out\" coffee, from the 1900s), im-primere pressing onto (imprint, impression — first a stamp, then an effect on the mind; print is its French clipping preinte, taken over for the new art after 1450), com-primere to press together (compress), de-primere to press down (depress; the economic sense settled in the 1930s), op-primere to bear down on (oppress), re-primere to press back (repress), sup-primere to press under (suppress), pressura pressure, pressa a pressing-machine — the printing press, and from the 1820s the newspapers."],
  am: ["amare(爱)与 amita(姑母)、amma(保姆)同源——可能来自儿语的 am-/ma-(如 mater)。罗马人的 amor 是爱情之神(Amor 即 Cupido),amicus 是朋友——\"被爱的人\"(西班牙语 amigo,法语 ami,amicable 友好的,enemy 是 in-imicus 不是朋友的人),amator 爱好者(amateur 是法语\"因为爱而做\"的人,18 世纪才带贬义),amabilis 可爱的(amiable、amiability),amorosus 多情的(amorous),Amanda 是\"该被爱的女子\",amour 法语的爱,enamoured 陷入爱,paramour 是 par amour(为了爱)——情人,amity 友好,Amadeus 是\"爱神的\"。",
    "amare (to love) is kin to amita (aunt) and amma (nurse) — probably from nursery babble am-/ma- (like mater). Roman Amor was the god of love (also Cupido); an amicus is a friend — one loved (Spanish amigo, French ami, amicable; an enemy is an in-imicus, a non-friend), an amator a lover of something (an amateur is French for one who does it for love, pejorative only since the 18th century), amabilis lovable (amiable), amorosus amorous, Amanda \"she who is to be loved\", amour French love, enamoured fallen in love, a paramour par amour, for love — a lover, amity friendship, Amadeus loved by God."],
  ocul: ["oculus(眼)来自印欧语 *h₃ekʷ-(看、眼),同根有希腊语 ops、英语 eye、德语 Auge、俄语 oko。罗马人的 oculus 也指建筑上的圆窗(万神殿顶的 oculus);ocularis 眼的(ocular),oculista 眼科医生,in-oculare 是\"把芽眼嫁接进去\"——园艺术语,1720 年代转指接种(inoculate),bin-oculus 双眼(binoculars),monocle 单片镜,ocelot 与此无关。oeil 法语眼、ojo 西班牙语、occhio 意大利语都是 oculus;ogle(色迷迷地看)是荷兰语的同根词;antler 是 ante-ocularis——\"眼前的\"鹿角(经法语),window 与此无关(古挪威语\"风眼\")。",
    "oculus (eye) comes from PIE *h₃ekʷ- (to see; eye), shared with Greek ops, English eye, German Auge, Russian oko. Roman oculus also named a round window (the oculus in the Pantheon's dome); ocularis of the eye (ocular), oculista an eye doctor, in-oculare was to graft in a bud-\"eye\" — a gardener's word that shifted to vaccination in the 1720s (inoculate), bin-oculus two-eyed (binoculars), a monocle a single glass. French oeil, Spanish ojo, Italian occhio are all oculus; ogle is a Dutch cousin; antler is ante-ocularis — the branch before the eye — through French; window is unrelated (Old Norse wind-eye)."],
  don: ["donum(礼物)与 dare(给)来自印欧语 *deh₃-(给),同根有希腊语 didōmi(dose 是\"给的一份\"、antidote 是\"给来对抗的\"、anecdote 是\"未发表的\")、梵语 dadāti、俄语 dat'。罗马人 donare 赠送(donate),dos(嫁妆,属格 dotis)是给新娘的(dowry、endow、dot 法语嫁妆),data 是\"给定的东西\"(datum,数据是 20 世纪义),datum 也是日期——信末写 datum Romae(于罗马给出),date 由此;tradere 交出(tradition 是传下来的,traitor 是交出城的人,treason 同源),red-dere 给回(render、rent 是给回的钱),ad-dere 加上(add),con-donare 一并给——宽恕(condone),per-dere 彻底给掉——失去(perdition),edere 发出(edit 出版),pardon 是 per-donare(完全给)——赦免,mandate 是手里给。",
    "donum (a gift) and dare (to give) come from PIE *deh₃- (to give), shared with Greek didōmi (a dose is a portion given, an antidote what is given against, an anecdote what was not given out — unpublished), Sanskrit dadāti, Russian dat'. Romans donare to present (donate), dos (dowry, genitive dotis) what is given with a bride (dowry, endow, French dot), data \"things given\" (datum; the computing sense is 20th-century), datum also a date — letters ended datum Romae, given at Rome, tradere to hand over (tradition is what is handed down, a traitor hands over the city, treason is the same word), red-dere to give back (render; rent is money given back), ad-dere to add, con-donare to give together — to condone, per-dere to give away entirely — to lose (perdition), edere to give out (edit), pardon is per-donare, to give wholly — to forgive."],
  numer: ["numerus(数)来自印欧语 *nem-(分配),同根有希腊语 nemein(分配——nomos 法律是\"分配的份\",nemesis 是分给你的报应)、德语 nehmen(拿)、哥特语 niman。罗马人 numerare 计数,numerus 也指军团的\"员额\";numerosus 众多(numerous),e-numerare 一一数出(enumerate),in-numerabilis 数不清,numerator 分子,numeral 数字,super-numerarius 员额外的(supernumerary),Numbers(民数记)是拉丁语 Liber Numerorum 的译名;number 经法语 nombre,numb 与此无关(古英语 niman 的分词——\"被拿走感觉的\")。",
    "numerus (a number) comes from PIE *nem- (to allot), shared with Greek nemein (to distribute — nomos, law, is the allotted share, nemesis what is dealt out to you), German nehmen (to take), Gothic niman. Romans numerare to count; numerus also meant a unit's full strength; numerosus many (numerous), e-numerare to count out one by one (enumerate), in-numerabilis countless, a numerator the counting part of a fraction, a numeral a figure, super-numerarius beyond the establishment (supernumerary); the book of Numbers translates Liber Numerorum; number comes through French nombre, while numb is unrelated (the participle of Old English niman — \"taken\", deprived of feeling)."],
  pen: ["poena(惩罚、赔偿)是拉丁语借自希腊语 poinē(血债、赎罪金)的词,印欧语 *kʷei-(赔偿、报复),同根有梵语 cayate(报复)、阿维斯陀语 kaēnā。罗马法的 poena 先是赔偿金,后指刑罚;poenalis 刑事的(penal),punire 惩罚(punish、punitive),poenitentia 是后悔(penitence,忏悔 penance 是它的法语形状),im-punitas 免罚(impunity),sub-poena 是\"在处罚之下\"传唤——传票,pain 是 poena 经法语 peine——先指刑罚,后指痛苦(pains 费心),penalty 罚,repent 后悔。",
    "poena (penalty, compensation) is Latin borrowed from Greek poinē (blood-price, ransom), PIE *kʷei- (to pay, avenge), shared with Sanskrit cayate (avenges), Avestan kaēnā. Roman poena was first a compensation, then a punishment; poenalis penal, punire to punish (punitive), poenitentia regret (penitence; penance is its French form), im-punitas freedom from punishment (impunity), sub-poena a summons \"under penalty\", pain is poena through French peine — first punishment, then suffering (to take pains), penalty, repent."],
  sec: ["secare(切)来自印欧语 *sek-(切),同根有英语 saw(锯)、scythe、sickle(拉丁语 secula 经借入)、德语 Säge、Sense,俄语 sech'。罗马人 secare 说切割、锯木、做手术;sectio 切开(section),sector 切下的扇形——扇区(sector),segmentum 切片(segment),in-sectum 是\"切成节的\"动物——昆虫(insect,亚里士多德 entoma 的直译),dis-secare 解剖(dissect),re-secare 切除(resect),secans 切线(secant),sect 与此无关(sequi),segment、sickle、scythe、saw 都是切。",
    "secare (to cut) comes from PIE *sek- (to cut), shared with English saw, scythe and sickle (Latin secula, borrowed), German Säge and Sense, Russian sech'. Romans secare cut, sawed and operated; sectio is a cutting (section), a sector the wedge cut out (sector), a segmentum a slice (segment), an in-sectum is the animal \"cut into\" segments — an insect (a translation of Aristotle's entoma), dis-secare to cut apart (dissect), re-secare to cut away (resect), secans a cutting line (secant); sect is unrelated (sequi). Segment, sickle, scythe and saw are all cutting."],
  und: ["unda(波、水)来自印欧语 *wed-(水),同根有英语 water、wet、otter(\"水兽\")、德语 Wasser、希腊语 hydōr(hydro-)、俄语 voda(vodka 是\"小水\")——拉丁语用鼻音中缀变成 unda。罗马人 undare 是起浪,ab-undare 是水漫出来——丰富(abundant),red-undare 水回涌——多余(redundant),in-undare 水灌进来——淹没(inundate),sur-round 是 super-undare(水漫过)经法语 suronder——本义淹没,16 世纪才因与 round 联想转为\"环绕\";undulare 起伏(undulate),undine 是帕拉塞尔苏斯造的水精。",
    "unda (a wave, water) comes from PIE *wed- (water), shared with English water, wet, otter (the water-beast), German Wasser, Greek hydōr (hydro-), Russian voda (vodka is \"little water\") — Latin added a nasal to make unda. Romans undare to surge; ab-undare is water running over — abundant, red-undare water surging back — redundant, in-undare water flooding in — inundate, surround is super-undare through French suronder — to overflow, which shifted to \"encircle\" in the 16th century by association with round; undulare to ripple (undulate); the undine is Paracelsus' water-spirit."],
  bolo: ["希腊语 ballein(扔)来自印欧语 *gʷelh₁-(扔、刺),同根有梵语 gulph(?)有争议、拉丁语 无直接对应。希腊人 ballein 说投石、投票(psēphon ballein)、也说\"抛出\"话:pro-blēma 是扔到前面的东西——障碍、问题,sym-ballein 扔到一起——symbolon 是折成两半、拼合作凭证的信物(symbol),dia-ballein 扔过去——诽谤(diabolos 中伤者——魔鬼,devil),em-blēma 嵌进去的饰片(emblem),para-bolē 并排扔——比喻(parable,parabola 是与圆锥面\"并置\"的曲线,palaver 和 parole、parler 都是它:说话就是打比方),hyper-bolē 扔过头——夸张、双曲线,meta-bolē 转变(metabolism),ballista 投石机,ball 舞会是 ballare(跳舞)另一词。",
    "Greek ballein (to throw) comes from PIE *gʷelh₁- (to throw, pierce), with no direct Latin cognate. Greeks ballein stones, ballots (psēphon ballein) and words: a pro-blēma is what is thrown before you — an obstacle, a problem, sym-ballein throwing together — a symbolon was a token broken in two and matched as proof (symbol), dia-ballein throwing across — slander (a diabolos is a slanderer — the devil), an em-blēma a piece thrown in — an inlay (emblem), para-bolē a throwing beside — a comparison (parable; a parabola is the curve \"set beside\" the cone, and palaver, parole and parler all descend from it: to speak is to compare), hyper-bolē a throw beyond — exaggeration, the hyperbola, meta-bolē a change (metabolism), a ballista a stone-thrower; a ball for dancing is ballare, another word."],
  dam: ["damnum(损失、罚金)可能来自印欧语 *deh₂p-(分、消耗),同根有希腊语 dapanē(花费)、英语 tame(?)有争议。罗马法的 damnum 是可诉的财产损失(《阿奎利亚法》damnum iniuria datum);damnare 是判处赔偿——定罪(damn 经法语 damner,基督教用它指永罚,骂人的 damn 是 16 世纪后的用法),con-demnare 定罪(condemn),in-demnis 无损的(indemnity 赔偿是\"使无损\"),damnosus 有害的,damage 是 damnaticum 经法语 dommage,in-dem-nify。",
    "damnum (loss, a fine) may come from PIE *deh₂p- (to divide, spend), shared with Greek dapanē (expense). Roman damnum was actionable loss to property (the lex Aquilia's damnum iniuria datum); damnare is to sentence to pay — to condemn (damn through French damner, taken by the Church for eternal punishment; the curse-word is post-16th-century), con-demnare to condemn, in-demnis unharmed (an indemnity makes one unharmed), damnosus harmful, damage is damnaticum through French dommage, indemnify to make whole."],
  flam: ["flamma(火焰)来自 flagrare(燃烧),印欧语 *bʰleg-(烧、闪),同根有希腊语 phlegein(烧——phlegm 是\"烧出来的\"体液,phlox 是火焰花)、英语 black(烧焦的)、blaze、bleach(烧白)。罗马的 flamen 祭司可能与此无关;flammare 点燃,in-flammare 点着——发炎(inflame、inflammation 是凯尔苏斯的 rubor et tumor cum calore),flammabilis 易燃,flamingo 是西班牙语\"火焰色的鸟\"(也说来自 Flemish 弗兰德人),flambé 法语\"点了火的\",flamboyant 是火焰式哥特建筑的\"火焰状\",con-flagratio 大火(conflagration),flagrante delicto 是\"罪行还在燃烧\"——当场抓获。",
    "flamma (flame) comes from flagrare (to blaze), PIE *bʰleg- (to burn, flash), shared with Greek phlegein (to burn — phlegm is the humour \"burnt out\" of the body, phlox the flame-flower), English black (charred), blaze, bleach (to burn white). flammare is to set alight, in-flammare to kindle — to inflame (inflammation is Celsus' redness and swelling with heat), flammabilis burnable, flamingo Spanish for the flame-coloured bird (or, some say, the Fleming), flambé French \"set alight\", flamboyant the flame-shaped tracery of late Gothic, con-flagratio a great fire (conflagration), flagrante delicto \"with the crime still blazing\" — caught in the act."],
  nunc: ["nuntius(信使、消息)可能来自 *noventius(\"报新闻的人\",与 novus 同根),也有人说来自 *neu-(点头、示意)。罗马元老院派 nuntii 传令;nuntiare 报告,an-nuntiare 宣布(announce;Annunciation 是天使报喜),de-nuntiare 正式通告——后指告发(denounce),pro-nuntiare 公开宣读——判决、发音(pronounce),re-nuntiare 报回——辞去、放弃(renounce),e-nuntiare 说出(enunciate),nuncio 是教廷大使(意大利语),internuncio 副使,Nunc dimittis(如今放走)是西面颂的首句。",
    "nuntius (a messenger, news) may be *noventius, \"the bringer of new things\" (kin to novus), or from *neu- (to nod, signal). The Roman senate sent nuntii; nuntiare to report, an-nuntiare to announce (the Annunciation is the angel's news to Mary), de-nuntiare to give formal notice — later to denounce, pro-nuntiare to read out in public — to pass sentence, to pronounce, re-nuntiare to report back — to resign, renounce, e-nuntiare to utter (enunciate); a nuncio is the pope's ambassador (Italian), an internuncio his deputy; Nunc dimittis, \"now you let depart\", opens the Song of Simeon."],
  chem: ["chemistry 的词源有两条线:一是希腊语 khymeia/khēmeia(\"熔合的技艺\"——从 khein 倒、khymos 汁),一是埃及的自称 Kmt(\"黑土之地\"),希腊人把埃及秘术叫 khēmia。阿拉伯人加定冠词成 al-kīmiyā(炼金术),12 世纪拉丁化为 alchemia;17 世纪波义耳等人去掉 al-,chemia/chymistry 成了新科学的名字。chemical 化学的,chemist 在英国指药剂师(阿拉伯-拉丁传统里炼金者调药),biochemistry 生物化学(1903),petrochemical 石油化学,alchemist 炼金术士,chemotherapy 是埃尔利希 1907 年造的\"化学疗法\"。",
    "Chemistry has two source lines: Greek khymeia/khēmeia, \"the art of fusing\" (from khein to pour, khymos juice), and Egypt's own name Kmt, \"the black land\", so that the Greeks called Egyptian secret arts khēmia. The Arabs added their article, al-kīmiyā (alchemy), Latinised in the 12th century as alchemia; in the 17th century Boyle and others dropped the al-, and chemia or chymistry named the new science. chemical, chemist (in Britain a pharmacist, since the alchemist compounded drugs), biochemistry (1903), petrochemical, alchemist, and chemotherapy Ehrlich's 1907 coinage."],
  host: ["hostis(陌生人→敌人)来自印欧语 *gʰostis(陌生人、客人),同根有英语 guest、德语 Gast、俄语 gost'——同一个词在日耳曼语里是客人,在拉丁语里变成了敌人(《十二表法》里 hostis 还指外邦人)。hospes(主人/客人)是 *gʰosti-pot-(\"对陌生人有权的人\"),给了 hospitalis 好客的(hospitable),hospitale 客房——中世纪的济贫院、医院(hospital),hospitium 招待(hospice 临终关怀所),hostel 和 hotel 是 hospitale 的两种法语磨损,host 主人经法语 hoste,hostage 是 hospitagium(被扣在\"客\"位的人),hostilis 敌对的(hostile),hostia 祭牲——圣体饼 host。",
    "hostis (stranger, then enemy) comes from PIE *gʰostis (stranger, guest), shared with English guest, German Gast, Russian gost' — one word that became guest in Germanic and enemy in Latin (in the Twelve Tables hostis still meant a foreigner). hospes (host/guest) is *gʰosti-pot-, \"one with power over strangers\", giving hospitalis hospitable, hospitale a guest-house — the medieval almshouse and hospital, hospitium entertainment (a hospice), hostel and hotel two French wearings of hospitale, host through French hoste, hostage hospitagium (one held in the guest's place), hostilis hostile, hostia a sacrificial victim — the host of the Mass."],
  grav: ["gravis(重)来自印欧语 *gʷerh₂-(重),同根有希腊语 barys(重——baro-meter、baritone)、梵语 guru(重、尊者——古鲁)、英语 无直接对应(quern 石磨是远亲)。罗马人 gravis 既是重量也是分量:gravitas 是罗马人推崇的\"庄重\"——政治家的品格;gravidus 怀孕的(\"负重的\"),ag-gravare 加重(aggravate;grieve 是它的法语磨损 grever,grief 悲伤),gravitas 在牛顿手里成了 gravity 万有引力(1665),grave 严重的,gravamen 诉状要点,grave 坟墓是另一个词(古英语)。",
    "gravis (heavy) comes from PIE *gʷerh₂- (heavy), shared with Greek barys (heavy — baro-meter, baritone), Sanskrit guru (weighty, venerable — a guru); English has no direct cognate (quern, the hand-mill, is a distant one). Roman gravis was weight and also weightiness: gravitas was the dignity Romans prized in a statesman; gravidus is pregnant (\"weighed down\"), ag-gravare to make heavier (aggravate; grieve is its French wearing grever, and grief), gravitas became Newton's gravity (1665), grave serious, gravamen the weight of a complaint; grave the tomb is another word (Old English)."],
  cert: ["certus(确定的)是 cernere(分辨)的古分词——\"已分辨清楚的\"。罗马人说 certus 是可靠的、确切的;certe 当然,certificare 是中世纪的\"使确定\"(certify、certificate),certitudo 确信(certitude),in-certus 不确定,certamen 是\"分出高下的比赛\"——竞赛(cernere 也指决斗),con-certare 一起争——法语 concerter 变成\"协调\",意大利语 concerto 是\"协调演奏\"(concert 音乐会 1600 年代),disconcert 打乱协调,ascertain 弄确实,certain 经法语 certain。",
    "certus (sure) is the old participle of cernere (to sift) — \"already sifted clear\". Roman certus is reliable, definite; certe of course, certificare medieval Latin \"to make certain\" (certify, certificate), certitudo certainty (certitude), in-certus uncertain, certamen a contest that decides the issue (cernere also meant to fight it out), con-certare to contend together — French concerter turned it into \"to arrange together\", Italian concerto into \"playing in agreement\" (a concert, 1600s), disconcert to upset the arrangement, ascertain to make sure, certain through French."],
  fend: ["-fendere(打击)只出现在复合词里,来自印欧语 *gʷʰen-(打、杀),同根有希腊语 theinein(打)、phonos(谋杀)、英语 bane(杀手——henbane 鸡毒草)、gun 与此无关。罗马人 de-fendere 是\"打开去\"——防御(defend、defence、fence 是 defence 的缩写——篱笆是防御物,fencing 击剑是防御术),of-fendere 是\"打上去\"——冒犯、绊到(offend、offence),fensa 给了 fend(挡开),fender 挡泥板。所以 fence 和 offence 都是打。",
    "-fendere (to strike) survives only in compounds, from PIE *gʷʰen- (to strike, kill), shared with Greek theinein (to strike), phonos (murder), English bane (a slayer — henbane); gun is unrelated. Roman de-fendere is striking away — to defend (defence; fence is defence clipped — a fence is a defence, and fencing the art of it), of-fendere striking against — to offend, to stumble (offence), fensa gave fend (to ward off) and fender. Fence and offence are both blows."],
  loqu: ["loqui(说)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能来自 *tolkʷ-(与俄语 tolk 意义有关?存疑)。罗马人 loqui 是日常说话(与 dicere 正式说、orare 演说相对);分词 locutus 给了 locutio 说法(locution),e-loquens 说得出来的——雄辩(eloquent),col-loquium 一起说——谈话(colloquial 口语的),soli-loquium 独白,circum-loquium 绕着说(circumlocution),inter-locutor 对话者,ventri-loquus 腹语者(ventriloquist,古人以为腹语来自肚子里的灵),grandi-loquus 说大话,ob-loquium 反着说——责难(obloquy),loquacious 多话的,elocution 演说术。",
    "loqui (to speak) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin. Roman loqui was everyday talk (as against dicere, formal saying, and orare, oratory); the participle locutus gave locutio a way of speaking (locution), e-loquens able to speak out — eloquent, col-loquium talking together (colloquial), soli-loquium talking alone, circum-loquium talking around (circumlocution), inter-locutor one who speaks between, ventri-loquus a belly-speaker (ventriloquist — ancients thought the voice came from a spirit in the belly), grandi-loquus big-talking, ob-loquium speaking against (obloquy), loquacious talkative, elocution the art of speaking."],
  cri: ["希腊语 krinein(分辨、判断)来自印欧语 *krei-(筛),与拉丁语 cernere 同根。希腊医生的 krisis 是病情的\"转折点\"——好转或死亡的判决时刻(crisis 的医学义先于政治义),kritēs 是裁判(critic 是 17 世纪从 kritikos\"善于判断的\"来的),kritērion 判断的标准(criterion),hypo-kritēs 是\"在面具下判断/回答的人\"——演员,基督教转为\"伪君子\"(hypocrite),dia-krinein 分辨(diacritic 区分符号),endocrine 内分泌是\"向内分出\",critique 法语的评论,criticize 1650 年代。",
    "Greek krinein (to separate, judge) comes from PIE *krei- (to sieve), kin to Latin cernere. For Greek doctors the krisis was the turning-point of an illness — the moment that decided recovery or death (the medical sense of crisis came before the political), a kritēs a judge (critic came in the 17th century from kritikos, skilled in judging), a kritērion a standard of judgement (criterion), a hypo-kritēs one who answers from under a mask — an actor, turned by Christians into a hypocrite, dia-krinein to distinguish (a diacritic distinguishes letters), endocrine \"separating inward\", critique the French review, criticize 1650s."],
  insul: ["insula(岛)词源不明——古人解释为 in salo(在海里),现代有人说与 *en-sl-(游泳)有关。罗马人的 insula 还指城里的公寓楼——被街道\"孤立\"出来的街区;insularis 岛的(insular 后指眼界狭窄),paen-insula 是\"几乎是岛\"(peninsula),isola 是意大利语岛——isolare 使成岛(isolate 1800 年前后从法语 isolé 借入,insulate 是同一个词的拉丁形——1530 年代指使成岛,1740 年代用于电学绝缘),insulin 是 1916 年从胰岛(insulae Langerhans)造的,isle 经法语 isle,island 与此无关(古英语 īegland,s 是仿 isle 加的)。",
    "insula (island) is of uncertain origin — ancients explained it as in salo, in the open sea; some moderns link it to *en-sl-, to swim. Roman insula also meant a city apartment block — a house-island cut off by streets; insularis of an island (insular, later narrow-minded), paen-insula \"almost an island\" (peninsula), Italian isola — isolare to make an island (isolate came around 1800 from French isolé; insulate is the same word in Latin dress — to make an island in the 1530s, electrical from the 1740s), insulin was coined in 1916 from the islets (insulae) of Langerhans, isle through French; island is unrelated (Old English īegland, its s added by imitation of isle)."],
  mor: ["mos(习俗、性格,属格 moris)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人的 mos maiorum(祖先的习俗)是共和国的不成文宪法;moralis 是西塞罗为译希腊语 ēthikos 造的词——\"关乎习俗的\"(moral、morality 是习俗的学问),morosus 是\"太讲究习惯的\"——阴沉、挑剔(morose),mores 至今是社会学的\"民俗规范\",demoralize 是法国大革命时的新词(1793),morale 士气是法语 moral 的英语再借。",
    "mos (custom, character, genitive moris) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. The mos maiorum, the way of the ancestors, was the Republic's unwritten constitution; moralis was Cicero's coinage to translate Greek ēthikos — \"pertaining to custom\" (moral; morality is the science of customs), morosus \"too set in his ways\" — sullen, particular (morose), mores is still the sociologist's word for a society's norms, demoralize a word of the French Revolution (1793), morale English reborrowing of French moral."],
  gnos: ["希腊语 gignōskein(认识)来自印欧语 *ǵneh₃-(知),与拉丁语 noscere、英语 know 同根。gnōsis 是\"知\"——2 世纪的诺斯替派(Gnostics)以秘传的知为救赎,a-gnōstos 不可知(agnostic 是赫胥黎 1869 年造的),dia-gnōsis 透彻辨知(diagnosis),pro-gnōsis 预知(prognosis,希波克拉底的名篇),gnōmōn 是\"知道的东西\"——日晷的指针(gnomon),gnōmē 是格言(gnomic),physio-gnomy 从相貌知人,i-gnorare 是拉丁语的\"不知\"(ignore 先指不知,19 世纪才指不理),i-gnominia 是\"没有好名\",notion、noble、cognition 在 not 条。",
    "Greek gignōskein (to know) comes from PIE *ǵneh₃- (to know), kin to Latin noscere and English know. gnōsis is knowledge — the 2nd-century Gnostics sought salvation through secret knowing, a-gnōstos unknowable (agnostic was Huxley's coinage of 1869), dia-gnōsis knowing through (diagnosis), pro-gnōsis knowing ahead (prognosis, a Hippocratic treatise), a gnōmōn \"the knower\" — the pointer of a sundial, a gnōmē a maxim (gnomic), physio-gnomy knowing a person from the face; Latin i-gnorare is not to know (ignore meant to be ignorant of before, in the 19th century, to disregard), i-gnominia loss of a good name; notion, noble and cognition sit under not."],
  epi: ["希腊语 epi(在上、在后)来自印欧语 *h₁epi(靠近、在上),同根有拉丁语 ob(对着)、梵语 api、英语 eaves(?)存疑。epi-dēmia 是\"在人民之上\"的病——希波克拉底用它指在一地流行的病(epidemic),epi-dermis 在皮肤上,epi-lēpsia 是\"被抓住\"(epilepsy 是\"神圣病\"),epi-sodion 是希腊悲剧合唱之间\"插进来\"的段落(episode),epi-taphios 墓上的话(epitaph),epi-logos 后话(epilogue),epi-phaneia 显现(epiphany),epi-stolē 送去的信(epistle),epi-skopos 监督者——主教(bishop 是 episcopus 磨损的),epi-tomē 切短的——概要(epitome),epi-centre 震源之上的点(1880 年代)。",
    "Greek epi (upon, after) comes from PIE *h₁epi (near, on), shared with Latin ob (against), Sanskrit api. epi-dēmia is a disease \"upon the people\" — Hippocrates' word for one prevalent in a place (epidemic), epi-dermis upon the skin, epi-lēpsia a seizing (epilepsy, the \"sacred disease\"), an epi-sodion the part \"coming in\" between choruses in tragedy (episode), epi-taphios words upon the tomb (epitaph), epi-logos words after (epilogue), epi-phaneia a showing (epiphany), epi-stolē a letter sent (epistle), epi-skopos an overseer — a bishop (bishop is episcopus worn down), epi-tomē a cutting short — a summary (epitome), epicentre the point above the focus (1880s)."],
  osis: ["-ōsis 是希腊语从 -oō 类动词造名词的后缀,表示\"过程、状态\":gen-esis 生成、hypn-osis 催眠(1840 年代 Braid 造)、osm-osis 推动(1867)、symbi-osis 共生(1877)、metamorph-osis 变形(奥维德的书名)、thromb-osis 凝块。医学把它专用于\"病态\":neur-osis(1777 年 Cullen 造)、psych-osis(1845)、tubercul-osis(1839)、cirrh-osis(1819,\"橙黄色的\")、scler-osis 硬化。diagn-osis 与 progn-osis 是\"知的过程\"。复数 -oses。",
    "-ōsis is the Greek suffix that makes nouns of process or state from verbs in -oō: gen-esis a becoming, hypn-osis sleep-inducing (Braid, 1840s), osm-osis a pushing (1867), symbi-osis living together (1877), metamorph-osis a changing of shape (Ovid's title), thromb-osis clotting. Medicine reserved it for morbid states: neur-osis (Cullen, 1777), psych-osis (1845), tubercul-osis (1839), cirrh-osis (1819, \"tawny\"), scler-osis hardening. Diagn-osis and progn-osis are processes of knowing. Plural -oses."],
  cid: ["caedere(砍、杀)来自印欧语 *keh₂id-(打),同根有梵语 khidati(撕)、日耳曼语可能无对应。在复合词里变 -cidere/-cisum:homi-cida 杀人者(homicide;-cide 成了\"杀\"的后缀:sui-cide 1650 年代、geno-cide 1944 年莱姆金造、insecti-cide、regi-cide 1590 年代为弑君造),de-cidere 是\"砍断\"——决定(decide,decision 是砍掉其他选项),prae-cidere 前面砍掉——精确(precise 是砍掉多余的),con-cisus 砍短的(concise),in-cidere 砍进去(incision、incisive),ex-cidere 砍掉(excise),circum-cidere 绕着砍(circumcise),caementum 是砍下的石屑——水泥 cement,chisel 是 caesellum 经法语,scissors 是 cisorium 的误拼(受 scindere 影响)。",
    "caedere (to cut, kill) comes from PIE *keh₂id- (to strike), shared with Sanskrit khidati (tears). In compounds it becomes -cidere/-cisum: a homi-cida is a man-killer (homicide; -cide became the suffix of killing: sui-cide 1650s, geno-cide Lemkin's 1944 coinage, insecti-cide, regi-cide made for king-killing in the 1590s), de-cidere is cutting off — to decide (a decision cuts away the alternatives), prae-cidere cutting in front — precise (the surplus cut off), con-cisus cut short (concise), in-cidere cutting into (incision, incisive), ex-cidere cutting out (excise), circum-cidere cutting around (circumcise), caementum stone chippings — cement, chisel is caesellum through French, scissors a misspelling of cisorium under the influence of scindere."],
  cit: ["ciere(使动、召唤)与 citare(频繁式:反复召唤)来自印欧语 *kei-(动),同根有希腊语 kinein(动——cinema、kinetic)、seiein(摇)。罗马法官 citare 是传唤证人;cito 快(citus 被推动的),citatio 传唤(citation 19 世纪才指引文——把作者\"传来\"作证),ex-citare 唤起(excite),in-citare 激起(incite),re-citare 从文本\"召回\"——朗读(recite),sus-citare 从下唤醒——re-suscitare 复活(resuscitate),sol-licitare 整个搅动——打扰、请求(solicit;solicitor 是替人请求的律师),cite 引用。",
    "ciere (to set in motion, summon) and its frequentative citare come from PIE *kei- (to move), shared with Greek kinein (to move — cinema, kinetic) and seiein (to shake). A Roman magistrate citare a witness — summoned him; cito is quickly (citus, set going), citatio a summons (citation meant a quoted passage only from the 19th century — the author \"summoned\" to testify), ex-citare to rouse (excite), in-citare to stir up (incite), re-citare to call back from the text — recite, sus-citare to rouse from beneath — re-suscitare to revive (resuscitate), sol-licitare to stir wholly — to trouble, to entreat (solicit; a solicitor entreats on your behalf), cite to quote."],
  pot: ["posse(能)是 potis(有能力的)+ esse(是)的合成;potis 来自印欧语 *poti-(主人、丈夫),同根有希腊语 posis(丈夫)、des-potēs(家主——despot)、梵语 pati(主)、立陶宛语 pats(自己)。所以\"能\"本是\"做主\":potens 有权势的(potent、potentate 统治者),potentia 力量(potency;potential 是经院哲学的\"潜在的\"——与 actual 相对),potestas 权力(法语 pouvoir 是 potere 的变形——power 由此),possibilis 可能的(possible),im-potens 无力的,omni-potens 全能,pot 锅是另一个词,hospes(主人)里的 -pes 也是 potis。",
    "posse (to be able) is potis (capable) + esse (to be); potis comes from PIE *poti- (master, husband), shared with Greek posis (husband), des-potēs (master of the house — despot), Sanskrit pati (lord), Lithuanian pats (self). So \"can\" was originally \"to be master\": potens powerful (potent; a potentate a ruler), potentia power (potency; potential is the schoolmen's \"in possibility\", opposed to actual), potestas authority (French pouvoir is potere reshaped — whence power), possibilis able to be (possible), im-potens powerless, omni-potens almighty; pot the vessel is another word, and the -pes of hospes (host) is potis too."],
  umbr: ["umbra(影)来自印欧语 *h₃end-(黑暗)存疑,同根的可能有立陶宛语 unksmė(影)。罗马人的 umbra 是树荫、影子、也是鬼魂(维吉尔笔下冥界的 umbrae)和\"陪客\"(蹭饭的人是主客带来的\"影子\");umbella 小伞——遮阳的(umbrella 经意大利语 ombrella,17 世纪传入英国时被嘲笑为娘娘腔),umbraculum 凉棚,ad-umbrare 打草稿——预示(adumbrate),pen-umbra 是开普勒 1604 年造的\"几乎是影\"——半影,umber 是\"翁布里亚土\"或\"影色\"(棕色颜料),sombre 是 sub-umbra 经法语——阴沉,sombrero 西班牙语\"遮阳帽\",umbrage 是被阴影笼罩——不快。",
    "umbra (shade) is of uncertain origin — perhaps PIE *h₃end- (dark), with Lithuanian unksmė (shade) a possible cousin. Roman umbra was shade, a shadow, a ghost (Virgil's umbrae of the underworld) and an uninvited guest brought along as a host's \"shadow\"; umbella a little shade (umbrella through Italian ombrella, mocked as effeminate when it reached 17th-century England), umbraculum an arbour, ad-umbrare to sketch in shadow — foreshadow (adumbrate), pen-umbra Kepler's 1604 \"almost-shadow\", umber the brown pigment (Umbrian earth or shadow-colour), sombre sub-umbra through French, a sombrero a shade-hat, umbrage the shadow cast — offence."],
  div: ["dividere(分)是 dis-(分开)+ *videre(分,与 viduus 丧偶的、英语 widow 同根,印欧语 *weidʰ-,分开)。罗马人 dividere 分战利品、分田;divisio 划分(division,军队的\"师\"是 18 世纪法国的用法),dividendum 是\"要分的数\"——被除数,后指股息(dividend),in-dividuus 是不可再分的——individual 从中世纪哲学的\"个体\"到 17 世纪的\"个人\",de-vise 是 dividere 经法语 deviser——分派、后指设计,device 设计出的东西,divisor 除数,dividers 分规。",
    "dividere (to divide) is dis- (apart) + *videre (to part, kin to viduus, bereaved, and English widow, PIE *weidʰ-, to separate). Romans dividere spoils and land; divisio a division (the military division is 18th-century French usage), dividendum the number to be divided — the dividend, later a share of profits, in-dividuus what cannot be divided further — individual, from the schoolmen's \"single thing\" to the 17th century's \"person\", devise is dividere through French deviser — to apportion, then to plan, a device a thing devised, divisor, dividers."],
  lact: ["lac(奶,属格 lactis)来自印欧语 *glakt-,同根有希腊语 gala(galaxy 是\"奶路\"——银河,galactic)、赫梯语?存疑。罗马人 lactare 哺乳(lactate),lactuca 是莴苣——因为流白汁(lettuce 经法语 laitue),lacteus 奶的(Via Lactea 银河),lactis 给了 latte(意大利语奶,caffè latte),lait 法语,leche 西班牙语(dulce de leche),lactose 乳糖(1843),ablactation 断奶,lactic acid 乳酸是舍勒 1780 年从酸奶里分离的。",
    "lac (milk, genitive lactis) comes from PIE *glakt-, shared with Greek gala (a galaxy is a milk-road — the Milky Way; galactic). Romans lactare to suckle (lactate), lactuca lettuce, for its milky sap (lettuce through French laitue), lacteus milky (the Via Lactea), and lactis gave Italian latte (caffè latte), French lait, Spanish leche (dulce de leche), lactose milk-sugar (1843), ablactation weaning; lactic acid was isolated from sour milk by Scheele in 1780."],
  medic: ["mederi(治疗)来自印欧语 *med-(量、思量、照料),同根有 modus(尺度)、meditari、希腊语 medomai(思虑)、Mēdeia(美狄亚——会药术的)、英语 mete。治病是\"量出合适的处置\";medicus 医生,medicina 医术(medicine),medicamentum 药物,re-medium 是\"重新治\"——补救(remedy),medicare 用药——meddle 与此无关(miscere),Medici 家族是\"医生们\"——佛罗伦萨的姓,medical 1640 年代,medic 军医,medicate 施药,premedication 术前用药。",
    "mederi (to heal) comes from PIE *med- (to measure, take counsel, care for), shared with modus (a measure), meditari, Greek medomai (to plan), Mēdeia (Medea, the one skilled in remedies), English mete. Healing is measuring out the fitting treatment; medicus a physician, medicina the art (medicine), medicamentum a drug, re-medium a healing again — a remedy, medicare to treat with drugs; meddle is unrelated (miscere); the Medici were \"the physicians\", a Florentine surname; medical 1640s, medic, medicate, premedication."],
  radi: ["radius(辐条、光线、半径)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人的 radius 是车轮的辐条、几何的半径、也是太阳射出的光线——三者都是\"从中心伸出的直线\";radiare 发光(radiate、radiant),radiatio 辐射(radiation;radium 是居里夫妇 1898 年命名的\"发光的\"),radio 是 radio-telegraphy 的缩写(1900 年代),radiator 散热器,radix(根)是另一个词但同样\"从中心伸出\":radical 是根本的(1790 年代政治义)、radish 萝卜、eradicate 连根拔、radicle 胚根,ray 是 radius 经法语 rai。",
    "radius (a spoke, a ray, a radius) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Roman radius was a wheel's spoke, a circle's radius and a sunbeam — all straight lines reaching out from a centre; radiare to shine out (radiate, radiant), radiatio radiation (radium was the Curies' 1898 \"radiant\" element), radio short for radio-telegraphy (1900s), radiator. radix (a root) is a separate word that likewise reaches out from a centre: radical is from the root (political since the 1790s), radish, eradicate to root out, radicle a seed-root; ray is radius through French rai."],
  san: ["sanus(健康的)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人的格言 mens sana in corpore sano(健全的心智在健全的身体里)出自尤维纳利斯;sanitas 健康(sanity 后专指心智健全,sanitation 是 19 世纪的公共卫生),sanare 治愈(sanatorium 疗养院是 1839 年的新拉丁语),in-sanus 不健全——疯(insane),sanitarius 卫生的(sanitary),sanguis(血)是另一个词,sane 神志清醒,sanative 有疗效。",
    "sanus (healthy) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Juvenal's mens sana in corpore sano — a sound mind in a sound body — is the famous phrase; sanitas health (sanity narrowed to the mind, sanitation is 19th-century public health), sanare to heal (a sanatorium is New Latin of 1839), in-sanus unsound — mad (insane), sanitarius sanitary; sanguis (blood) is another word; sane, sanative."],
  cast: ["castus(纯洁的、切除的)来自印欧语 *kes-(切),同根有 carere(缺少——\"被切掉\")、castrare(阉割)、希腊语 keazein(劈)。罗马人的 castus 是仪式上洁净的;castitas 贞洁(chastity),castigare 是\"使纯\"——惩戒(castigate;chastise 经法语),in-cestus 不洁——乱伦(incest),caste 是葡萄牙语 casta(纯种)——16 世纪用于印度的种姓。castellum(堡)是 castrum(营地)的小型:castra 是军营(Chester、Lancaster、Manchester 的 -caster/-chester 都是它),castle 经诺曼法语,château 法语,Castile 卡斯蒂利亚是\"城堡之地\"。两个 cast 不同源。",
    "castus (pure, cut off) comes from PIE *kes- (to cut), shared with carere (to lack — \"cut off from\"), castrare (to castrate), Greek keazein (to split). Roman castus meant ritually clean; castitas chastity, castigare to make pure — to correct (castigate; chastise through French), in-cestus impure — incest, caste is Portuguese casta, pure breed, applied to India in the 16th century. castellum (a fort) is the diminutive of castrum, a camp: castra is the army camp (the -caster/-chester of Chester, Lancaster, Manchester), castle came through Norman French, château is French, Castile the land of castles. The two cast- are unrelated."],
  foc: ["focus(炉灶)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马家庭的 focus 是灶火,家神拉尔就供在灶边;晚期拉丁语 focus 取代 ignis 成了\"火\"——法语 feu、意大利语 fuoco、西班牙语 fuego 都是 focus 而非 ignis。开普勒 1604 年借 focus 指透镜的\"燃点\"——焦点(focus 的现代义),焦距、聚焦、focal 随之而来;foyer 是法语的\"炉边\"——门厅,fuel 是 focalia(炉子的柴)经法语,fusil 步枪是 focile(打火石),curfew 是 couvre-feu(盖火——晚上熄火的钟声)。",
    "focus (a hearth) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. The Roman family's focus was the hearth-fire, with the household gods beside it; in late Latin focus replaced ignis as the word for fire — French feu, Italian fuoco, Spanish fuego are all focus. Kepler in 1604 borrowed focus for the \"burning point\" of a lens — the modern focus, with focal length and focusing to follow; a foyer is French for hearth-side — a lobby, fuel is focalia, hearth-wood, through French, a fusil is a focile, a fire-steel, and curfew is couvre-feu, cover-the-fire — the evening bell."],
  fruct: ["fructus(享用、果实)来自 frui(享受),印欧语 *bʰreug-(享用),同根有英语 brook(古义\"享用、忍受\")、德语 brauchen(需要、使用)、哥特语 brukjan。罗马法的 usus fructus 是使用收益权;fructus 是土地的收益——果实,fruitful 多产,frugalis 是\"有收成的\"——节俭(frugal),fruition 是\"享用\"(与 fruit 无关地被理解为结果),frumentum 谷物,frutta 意大利语,fruit 经法语,fructose 果糖(1857)。",
    "fructus (enjoyment, fruit) comes from frui (to enjoy), PIE *bʰreug- (to make use of), shared with English brook (in its old sense, to enjoy, endure), German brauchen (to need, use), Gothic brukjan. In Roman law usus fructus is the right to use and enjoy; fructus is what land yields — its fruit, fruitful, frugalis \"yielding fruit\" — thrifty (frugal), fruition is enjoyment (reinterpreted as fulfilment by association with fruit), frumentum grain, Italian frutta, fruit through French, fructose (1857)."],
  lun: ["luna(月亮)是 *louksna(\"发光的\")的缩合,来自 *leuk-(光),与 lux 同根;同根词有阿维斯陀语 raoxšna、古教会斯拉夫语 luna。罗马人的 dies Lunae 是星期一(法语 lundi、意大利语 lunedì、西班牙语 lunes);lunaris 月的(lunar),lunaticus 是\"受月亮影响的\"——疯(lunatic;古人认为月相引发癫狂),luna 也是炼金术的银,lunula 小月牙(指甲根的白弧),lunette 法语小月——半圆窗、眼镜(lunettes),Luna 女神,demi-lune 半月堡。",
    "luna (moon) is *louksna, \"the shining one\", contracted, from *leuk- (light), kin to lux; cognates include Avestan raoxšna and Old Church Slavonic luna. Roman dies Lunae is Monday (French lundi, Italian lunedì, Spanish lunes); lunaris lunar, lunaticus \"moon-struck\" — mad (lunatic; the phases were thought to trigger madness), luna also alchemists' silver, lunula a little crescent (the white arc at the nail's base), French lunette a little moon — a half-round window, and lunettes spectacles, the goddess Luna, a demi-lune a half-moon outwork."],
  tard: ["tardus(慢)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人 tardare 使迟缓;tarditas 迟缓,re-tardare 使推迟(retard;retardation 1420 年代指延迟,20 世纪一度用于智力,现已弃用),tardy 迟到的,tard 法语\"晚\"(à plus tard 回头见),tarde 西班牙语的下午(\"晚的时候\",buenas tardes),tardi 意大利语晚,ritardando 是乐谱上的\"渐慢\",retardant 阻燃剂。",
    "tardus (slow) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Romans tardare to delay; tarditas slowness, re-tardare to hold back (retard; retardation meant delay from the 1420s, was applied to intellect in the 20th century and is now avoided), tardy late, French tard \"late\" (à plus tard, see you later), Spanish tarde the afternoon (\"the late time\", buenas tardes), Italian tardi late, ritardando the score's \"slowing down\", a retardant."],
  verb: ["verbum(词)来自印欧语 *werh₁-(说),同根有英语 word、德语 Wort、希腊语 eirein(说)、rhētōr(演说家——rhetoric)。罗马语法家把 verbum 当作\"词类之一\"——动词(因为动词是句子的核心\"词\");verbalis 词的(verbal),verbatim 逐字,verbosus 话多(verbose),pro-verbium 是\"提出来的话\"——谚语(proverb),ad-verbium 加在动词上的(adverb),verbum 在《约翰福音》拉丁本里译 logos(In principio erat Verbum),verve 是法语的\"言辞之势\"——活力,verbiage 废话。",
    "verbum (a word) comes from PIE *werh₁- (to speak), shared with English word, German Wort, Greek eirein (to say) and rhētōr (a speaker — rhetoric). Roman grammarians made verbum the name of one part of speech — the verb, the sentence's central word; verbalis of words (verbal), verbatim word for word, verbosus wordy (verbose), pro-verbium a word put forward — a proverb, ad-verbium a word added to the verb (adverb); the Vulgate renders John's logos as Verbum (In principio erat Verbum), verve is French for force of words — spirit, verbiage excess of words."],
  vot: ["votum(誓愿)是 vovere(许愿)的分词——向神许下\"若你助我则我献…\"的誓:罗马将军出征前 vovere 一座神庙,凯旋后还愿。votum 后指愿望、也指表决时说出的意见——vote(15 世纪);votivus 还愿的(votive 蜡烛),de-votus 是\"献出去的\"——罗马军官 devotio 是把自己献给冥神换取胜利(devote、devout 虔诚是\"献了身的\"),vow 是 votum 经法语 vou,vouch 与此无关(vocare),avow 是 ad-votare 经法语。",
    "votum (a vow) is the participle of vovere (to vow) — the promise to a god, \"if you help me I will give…\": a Roman general vowed a temple before battle and paid it after his triumph. votum then meant a wish, and the opinion voiced in an assembly — a vote (15th century); votivus in fulfilment of a vow (votive candles), de-votus \"given over\" — a Roman officer's devotio offered his own life to the gods below for victory (devote; devout means self-dedicated), vow is votum through French vou, avow is ad-votare through French; vouch is unrelated (vocare)."],
  cap4: ["caput(头,属格 capitis)来自印欧语 *kaput-,同根有英语 head(古英语 hēafod)、德语 Haupt、梵语 kaput。罗马人的 caput 是人头、也是\"人\"本身:capitis deminutio 是丧失公民身份,capitalis 是\"关乎头的\"——死刑(capital punishment),capitale 是牲畜的\"头数\"——本钱、财产(capital 资本,cattle 与 chattel 是同一个词的两种法语形状!),capitulum 小头——章节(chapter 经法语 chapitre;capitulate 是分章拟定投降条款),capitaneus 首领(captain、chieftain、chef、chief 都是它),praecipitare 头朝下——坠落(precipitate),de-capitare 斩首,cap 帽经晚期拉丁语 cappa,cape 斗篷同源,escape 是 ex-cappa(脱下斗篷跑掉),capitol 是罗马的\"头山\"神庙——华盛顿仿之,cabbage 是\"头菜\"(法语 caboche),biceps 双头肌。",
    "caput (head, genitive capitis) comes from PIE *kaput-, shared with English head (Old English hēafod), German Haupt, Sanskrit kaput. Roman caput was the head and the person himself: capitis deminutio was loss of civic status, capitalis \"concerning the head\" — capital punishment, capitale the count of heads of livestock — stock, wealth (capital; cattle and chattel are one word in two French shapes!), capitulum a little head — a chapter (through French chapitre; to capitulate is to draw up surrender terms in chapters), capitaneus a head man (captain, chieftain, chef, chief), praecipitare to go head first — precipitate, de-capitare to behead, cap through late Latin cappa, cape the same, escape ex-cappa — slipping out of the cloak, Capitol Rome's \"head hill\" temple, copied in Washington, cabbage the head-vegetable (French caboche), biceps two-headed."],
  myst: ["希腊语 myein(闭上眼或嘴)可能是拟声的(mu——闭唇音)。mystēs 是\"闭嘴的人\"——秘教入会者(厄琉西斯秘仪的信徒发誓不泄露所见),mystērion 是秘仪、秘密(mystery;《新约》用它指神的隐秘计划,拉丁语译 sacramentum),mystikos 秘仪的(mystic、mystical),mysticism 19 世纪,mystify 法语 mystifier(1770 年代),myopia 近视是 my-ōps\"闭眼\"——眯眼看。中世纪英语 mystery 还指行会(那是 ministerium 的混淆——mystery plays 是行会演的圣剧)。",
    "Greek myein (to close the eyes or lips) is probably imitative (mu — the closed-lip sound). A mystēs is \"one who keeps his mouth shut\" — an initiate (the Eleusinian initiates swore never to tell what they saw), a mystērion a secret rite, a secret (mystery; the New Testament uses it for God's hidden plan, Latin sacramentum), mystikos of the rites (mystic, mystical), mysticism 19th-century, mystify French mystifier (1770s), myopia is my-ōps, \"shut-eye\" — squinting. Middle English mystery also meant a craft guild (a confusion with ministerium — the mystery plays were the guilds' sacred dramas)."],
  mult: ["multus(多)可能来自印欧语 *mel-(强、大),同根有希腊语 mala(很)、拉脱维亚语 milns(很多)。罗马人 multitudo 是众多、群众(multitude),multi-plex 多折(multiple),multi-plicare 使成倍(multiply),multi-farius 多方面,multi-formis 多形,multa 是罚金(是另一个词,奥斯坎语借词),multi- 在现代是最多产的前缀之一:multi-national(1926)、multi-media(1962)、multi-tasking,molto 意大利语\"很\",mucho 西班牙语是 multum 经音变,mouth 与此无关。",
    "multus (much, many) may come from PIE *mel- (strong, great), shared with Greek mala (very) and Latvian milns (many). Roman multitudo is a great number, a crowd (multitude), multi-plex many-folded (multiple), multi-plicare to make manifold (multiply), multi-farius many-sided, multi-formis many-shaped; multa, a fine, is another word, borrowed from Oscan; multi- is among the most productive modern prefixes: multi-national (1926), multi-media (1962), multi-tasking; Italian molto is \"very\", Spanish mucho is multum reshaped; mouth is unrelated."],
  lud: ["ludus(游戏、学校)来自印欧语 *leid-(玩)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 loidoros(嘲骂)。罗马人的 ludi 是公共竞技会(ludi Romani、ludi circenses——面包与竞技),ludus 也是角斗士学校和小学(ludus litterarius);ludere 玩,al-ludere 玩到边上——暗指(allude、allusion),col-ludere 一起玩——串通(collude),de-ludere 玩弄——欺骗(delude、delusion),e-ludere 玩着躲开(elude),il-ludere 嘲弄(illusion 幻觉是 14 世纪转义),prae-ludium 前奏(prelude),inter-ludium 幕间(interlude),ludicrous 是\"游戏的\"——可笑(18 世纪转贬),ludic 游戏性的(1940 年代)。",
    "ludus (a game, a school) may come from PIE *leid- (to play); Greek loidoros (mocking) is a possible cousin. Roman ludi were the public games (ludi Romani, ludi circenses — bread and circuses), and a ludus was also a gladiator school and a primary school (ludus litterarius); ludere to play, al-ludere to play at the edge — to allude (allusion), col-ludere to play together — collude, de-ludere to play false — delude (delusion), e-ludere to play out of reach — elude, il-ludere to mock (illusion shifted to a false perception in the 14th century), prae-ludium a fore-play (prelude), inter-ludium a between-play (interlude), ludicrous \"playful\" turned to laughable in the 18th century, ludic (1940s)."],
  paed: ["希腊语 pais(孩子,属格 paidos)来自印欧语 *peh₂u-(小、少),同根有拉丁语 paucus(少)、puer(男孩)、pullus(幼崽)、英语 few、foal。paid-agōgos 是领孩子上学的奴隶(pedagogue),paid-eia 是教育——希腊文明的核心概念,en-kyklios paideia 全面教育——encyclopaedia(1530 年代),paid-iatros 儿科医生(1880 年代),orthopaedic 是安德里 1741 年造的\"矫正儿童\",pedant 是意大利语 pedante(教书匠),paedophile 1906 年,pediatrics 美式拼写,page(侍童)可能是 paidion 小孩经意大利语。",
    "Greek pais (child, genitive paidos) comes from PIE *peh₂u- (small, few), shared with Latin paucus (few), puer (boy), pullus (a young animal), English few and foal. A paid-agōgos was the slave who led the child to school (pedagogue), paid-eia was education — the central Greek idea, en-kyklios paideia all-round education — encyclopaedia (1530s), a paid-iatros a children's doctor (1880s), orthopaedic Andry's 1741 \"child-straightening\", a pedant Italian pedante, a schoolmaster, paedophile 1906, pediatrics the American spelling; page, the boy servant, may be paidion, a little child, through Italian."],
  imag: ["imago(像)来自印欧语 *h₂eim-(模仿)存疑,同根有 imitari(模仿)、aemulus(竞争者——emulate)。罗马贵族家里陈列祖先的蜡面 imagines,葬礼上有人戴着走在前面;imago 也是灵魂的影像、诗人的比喻。imaginari 在心里成像(imagine、imagination),imaginatio 是西塞罗译希腊语 phantasia 的词,imago 的法语形状 image,imagerie 图像集,imitatio 模仿(imitate),in-imitabilis 不可模仿,emulate 是 aemulari——模仿着争胜,imaginary number 是笛卡尔 1637 年的嘲讽用语。",
    "imago (a likeness) may come from PIE *h₂eim- (to copy), shared with imitari (to imitate) and aemulus (a rival — emulate). A Roman noble house displayed wax imagines of its ancestors, worn by actors walking before the funeral; imago was also the soul's shade and the poet's figure. imaginari is to form an image in the mind (imagine, imagination — Cicero's word for Greek phantasia), the French form image, imagerie a set of images, imitatio imitation, in-imitabilis inimitable, emulate is aemulari — to imitate in rivalry, imaginary numbers were Descartes' mocking term of 1637."],
  eco: ["希腊语 oikos(家、家产)来自印欧语 *weik-(住处),同根有拉丁语 vicus(村)、梵语 veśa、英语 -wick、哥特语 weihs。oiko-nomia 是\"管家之法\"——色诺芬的《家政论》,罗马人借为 oeconomia;economy 从家政到国家理财(political economy,1615 年蒙克莱田),economics 1790 年代;oiko-logia 是海克尔 1866 年造的\"栖息地之学\"——生态学(ecology),ecosystem 1935,eco- 前缀 1960 年代大量出现(eco-friendly),oiku-menē 是\"有人住的世界\"——ecumenical 普世的,parish 是 par-oikia\"住在旁边的人\"——教区,diocese 是 di-oikēsis 管理区。",
    "Greek oikos (a house, a household) comes from PIE *weik- (a dwelling), shared with Latin vicus (a village), Sanskrit veśa, English -wick, Gothic weihs. oiko-nomia is the rule of the household — Xenophon's Oeconomicus, borrowed by Rome as oeconomia; economy went from housekeeping to the finances of the state (political economy, Montchrétien 1615), economics in the 1790s; oiko-logia was Haeckel's 1866 \"science of habitat\" — ecology, ecosystem 1935, the eco- prefix proliferating from the 1960s (eco-friendly), oiku-menē the inhabited world — ecumenical, a parish par-oikia, \"those dwelling beside\", a diocese di-oikēsis, an administration."],
  hod: ["希腊语 hodos(路)来自印欧语 *sed-(走,与\"坐\"的 *sed- 有争议),同根有古教会斯拉夫语 choditi(走)、俄语 khod。meth-odos 是\"顺着的路\"——方法(柏拉图、亚里士多德的术语,拉丁语 methodus),ex-odos 出路——出埃及(Exodus),peri-odos 绕一圈的路——周期、句号(period),syn-odos 一起走——会议(synod),epis-odion 途中插入的,cath-ode/an-ode 是法拉第 1834 年造的\"下路/上路\"——阴极阳极(electrode 电极),odometer 里程表(1790 年代),hodograph。",
    "Greek hodos (a way, road) comes from PIE *sed- (to go), shared with Old Church Slavonic choditi (to walk), Russian khod. meth-odos is the way along — a method (Plato's and Aristotle's term, Latin methodus), ex-odos the way out — the Exodus, peri-odos the way round — a period, a full stop, syn-odos a going together — a synod, epis-odion what enters on the way, cath-ode and an-ode Faraday's 1834 \"down-way\" and \"up-way\" (electrode), odometer a way-measurer (1790s), hodograph."],
  crim: ["crimen(指控、罪)来自 cernere(分辨、判决)——本义\"判决的对象\"、指控;印欧语 *krei-(筛)。在古典拉丁语里 crimen 主要是\"控告\"(西塞罗的 crimen 是对方的指控),\"罪行\"义是后起的;criminalis 刑事的(criminal 18 世纪才指罪犯),dis-criminare 分辨(discriminate 先是区分,1866 年美国才有歧视义),re-criminatio 反控(recrimination),in-criminare 控告(incriminate),criminology 1890,crime 经法语。",
    "crimen (an accusation, a crime) comes from cernere (to sift, decide) — originally the matter decided, the charge; PIE *krei- (to sieve). In classical Latin crimen mostly meant the accusation (Cicero's crimen is the other side's charge); the sense of the deed itself came later; criminalis of charges (criminal meant a wrongdoer only from the 18th century), dis-criminare to distinguish (discriminate meant to tell apart until 1866, when American usage added prejudice), re-criminatio a counter-charge (recrimination), in-criminare to charge (incriminate), criminology 1890, crime through French."],
  fam: ["fama(传言、名声)来自 fari(说),印欧语 *bʰeh₂-,同根有希腊语 phēmē(传言)、phanai。维吉尔笔下 Fama 是长满眼睛和舌头的怪物,越传越大;famosus 先是\"被谈论的\"——中性,后分化为 famous(有名)和 infamous(臭名;in-famis 是\"没有好名声\",罗马法的 infamia 剥夺荣誉权),de-famare 说人坏话(defame),fame 经法语,famiglia 与此无关(famulus 仆人——family 本义\"一家的奴仆\"),fable、fate、infant 在 fat 条。",
    "fama (report, reputation) comes from fari (to speak), PIE *bʰeh₂-, shared with Greek phēmē (rumour) and phanai. Virgil's Fama is a monster covered in eyes and tongues that grows as she travels; famosus was first \"talked about\" — neutral, then split into famous and infamous (in-famis is without good name; Roman infamia stripped a man of civic honour), de-famare to speak ill of (defame), fame through French; family is unrelated (famulus, a servant — a familia was the household's slaves); fable, fate and infant sit under fat."],
  ol: ["olere(发气味)来自印欧语 *h₃ed-(闻),同根有希腊语 ozein(发臭——ozone 是 1840 年舍恩拜因造的\"有气味的气\")、osmē(气味)、亚美尼亚语 hot;拉丁语的 d 变成了 l(古拉丁语 odere)。odor 气味(odour;deodorant 1867),odoratus 嗅觉,red-olere 散发(redolent),ol-factus 是\"用嗅闻做\"——嗅觉(olfactory),ad-olere 焚香——adolescere\"长大\"的 ol- 是另一个根(alere 养),malodorous 恶臭,odorous 香。",
    "olere (to give off a smell) comes from PIE *h₃ed- (to smell), shared with Greek ozein (to stink — ozone was Schönbein's 1840 \"smelling gas\"), osmē (odour), Armenian hot; Latin turned the d into l (Old Latin odere). odor a smell (odour; deodorant 1867), odoratus the sense of smell, red-olere to give off scent (redolent), ol-factus \"made by smelling\" — olfactory; the ol- of adolescere (to grow up) is a different root (alere, to nourish); malodorous, odorous."],
  quot: ["quot(多少)来自印欧语 *kʷo-(疑问词干,与 who、what、quis 同根)加 -ti。罗马人问 quot? 多少;quotus 第几,quota 是中世纪拉丁语 quota pars(多大份)——配额,quotidianus 每日的(quotidian;quotidian fever 每日热),quotiens 多少次——quotient 商,quotare 是给书页标号,后指\"按号引用\"——quote(16 世纪),quotation 引文、报价,aliquot 若干(整除的),quorum 是\"其中的哪些人\"——法定人数(委任状里的用语 quorum vos … unum esse volumus)。",
    "quot (how many) comes from PIE *kʷo- (the interrogative stem, kin to who, what, quis) plus -ti. Romans asked quot?; quotus is which in order, quota medieval quota pars, how large a share (a quota), quotidianus daily (quotidian; a quotidian fever recurs each day), quotiens how many times — a quotient, quotare was to number the pages of a book, then to cite by number — to quote (16th century), a quotation a passage or a price, aliquot a certain number (dividing evenly), quorum \"of whom\" — the minimum members, from the wording of commissions, quorum vos … unum esse volumus."],
  sid: ["sidus(星座,属格 sideris)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——与 stella(单颗星)相对,sidus 是一群星。罗马人 con-siderare 本是占卜术语:仔细观察星象——考虑(consider),de-siderare 是\"从星象上看不到\"——想念、渴望(desire;desideratum 所缺之物),sideralis 星的(sidereal 恒星的),sideratio 是星辰引起的麻痹——中风,sideroxylon 与此无关(希腊语 sidēros 铁)。所以 consider 和 desire 都出自看星星。",
    "sidus (a constellation, genitive sideris) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — as against stella, a single star, sidus was a group. Roman con-siderare began as augury: to study the stars closely — to consider, de-siderare \"to miss from the stars\" — to long for (desire; a desideratum is what is missing), sideralis of the stars (sidereal), sideratio a star-struck paralysis — a stroke; sideroxylon is unrelated (Greek sidēros, iron). Consider and desire both come from star-gazing."],
  ton: ["希腊语 tonos(拉紧、弦、音调)来自 teinein(拉),印欧语 *ten-,与拉丁语 tendere、英语 thin 同根。希腊音乐里 tonos 是琴弦的张力决定的音高(tone),罗马人借为 tonus;tonicus 是\"关于张力的\"——tonic 在医学里是使肌肉有张力的补药,在音乐里是主音(tonic),mono-tonos 一个音(monotonous),bary-tonos 重音(baritone),tonsil 与此无关,in-tonare 起调(intone、intonation),a-tonia 无张力(atony),de-tonare 是拉丁语 tonare(打雷)——引爆(detonate),tune 是 tone 的英语变体(14 世纪),astonish 是 ex-tonare(被雷打到)经法语。",
    "Greek tonos (a stretching, a string, a pitch) comes from teinein (to stretch), PIE *ten-, kin to Latin tendere and English thin. In Greek music tonos was the pitch set by a string's tension (tone), borrowed by Rome as tonus; tonicus \"concerning tension\" — a tonic in medicine restores muscle tone, in music it is the key-note, mono-tonos one tone (monotonous), bary-tonos deep-toned (baritone), in-tonare to strike up (intone, intonation), a-tonia lack of tone (atony); de-tonare is Latin tonare, to thunder — to detonate, tune is an English variant of tone (14th century), astonish is ex-tonare, thunder-struck, through French; tonsil is unrelated."],
  veh: ["vehere(运载)来自印欧语 *weǵʰ-(运、驾车),同根有英语 wagon、way、weigh(古义\"运\")、德语 Weg、Wagen、bewegen、梵语 vahati。罗马人 vehere 用车运,vehiculum 车辆(vehicle),vexare 是频繁式\"反复摇晃\"——折磨(vex),vector 是\"载体\"——数学向量(1846 哈密顿)、病媒,via 道路是同根(via 的 v- 就是 vehere:能行车的路),con-vexus 是\"一起载着\"——凸(convex),ad-vehere 运来——advection,in-vehi 冲进——invective 是\"冲着人来的话\",con-voy 是法语 convoier(一同运送)——护送,convey 同源,weigh 与 wagon 是它的日耳曼亲戚,velox 快、velocity 也来自这个根。",
    "vehere (to carry) comes from PIE *weǵʰ- (to carry, drive), shared with English wagon, way and weigh (once \"to carry\"), German Weg, Wagen, bewegen, Sanskrit vahati. Romans vehere by cart; vehiculum a vehicle, vexare the frequentative \"to keep shaking\" — to torment (vex), vector a carrier — Hamilton's mathematical vector (1846), a disease-carrier, via a road is the same root (a way a cart can take), con-vexus \"carried together\" — convex, ad-vehere to carry to (advection), in-vehi to drive against — invective is speech driven at a person, convoy is French convoier, to carry along — to escort, convey the same; weigh and wagon are its Germanic cousins, and velox, velocity also spring from it."],
  vin: ["vinum(酒)与 vitis(藤)同根:印欧语 *wei-(缠绕)——葡萄是缠绕的藤;或者是地中海古语借词(希腊语 oinos、赫梯语 wiyana、亚美尼亚语 gini、格鲁吉亚语 ghvino 都相近,葡萄酒发源于高加索)。罗马人 vinea 葡萄园(vineyard 是 vine 加 yard),vinarius 酒商,vin-demia 摘葡萄——收获(vintage 经法语 vendange,vintage 指年份是 18 世纪),vinum acre 酸酒——醋(vinegar),vinosus 醉酒的,wine 是日耳曼语早期从拉丁借的(德语 Wein),vino 意大利语、vin 法语,oenology 是希腊线的酿酒学,vinyl 是\"酒的\"——乙烯基(1863,从乙醇得名)。",
    "vinum (wine) shares its root with vitis (the vine): PIE *wei- (to wind) — the grape twines; or both are loans from an old Mediterranean tongue (Greek oinos, Hittite wiyana, Armenian gini, Georgian ghvino all match, and wine-making began in the Caucasus). Roman vinea is a vineyard (vine + yard in English), vinarius a wine-merchant, vin-demia the grape-gathering (vintage through French vendange; the year-sense is 18th-century), vinum acre sour wine — vinegar, vinosus tipsy; wine was borrowed early into Germanic (German Wein), Italian vino, French vin, oenology the Greek line's science of wine, vinyl \"of wine\" — named from ethyl alcohol in 1863."],
  polit: ["希腊语 polis(城邦)来自印欧语 *pelh₁-(城堡、高地),同根有梵语 pur(城——Singapur 狮城)、立陶宛语 pilis(城堡)。polis 是希腊人的政治单位——公民共同体;politēs 公民,politeia 公民身份、政体(柏拉图《理想国》原名 Politeia),politikos 关乎城邦的——亚里士多德的《政治学》(politics 1520 年代),polit-ician 1580 年代,policy 是 politia 经法语 police——先指治理,再分化为 policy(政策)与 police(警察,18 世纪),polite 是 politus(打磨过的)——另一个词,metro-polis 母城,acro-polis 高城,cosmo-polis 世界城市,Naples 是 Nea-polis 新城,Constantinople 君士坦丁之城。",
    "Greek polis (city-state) comes from PIE *pelh₁- (citadel, high place), shared with Sanskrit pur (city — Singapur, lion city) and Lithuanian pilis (castle). The polis was the Greek political unit — the community of citizens; politēs a citizen, politeia citizenship, a constitution (Plato's Republic is the Politeia), politikos of the polis — Aristotle's Politics (politics 1520s), politician 1580s, policy is politia through French police — first governance, then split into policy and police (18th century), polite is politus, polished — another word; metro-polis the mother city, acro-polis the high city, cosmo-polis the world city, Naples is Nea-polis, new city, Constantinople Constantine's city."],
  sanguin: ["sanguis(血,属格 sanguinis)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马人区分 sanguis(流动的活血)与 cruor(流出凝固的血);sanguineus 血的、血红的(sanguine 是体液说里的\"多血质\"——乐观红润,15 世纪起),sanguinarius 嗜血的(sanguinary),con-sanguineus 同血的——血亲(consanguinity),ex-sanguis 无血的(exsanguinate),sangre 西班牙语、sangue 意大利语、sang 法语(sang-froid 冷血——镇定),sanguinolent 带血的,sangria 是西班牙语\"放血\"——血红的酒。",
    "sanguis (blood, genitive sanguinis) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Romans distinguished sanguis, the living blood in the veins, from cruor, blood shed and clotting; sanguineus bloody, blood-red (sanguine in humoral theory is the blood-rich temperament — cheerful and ruddy, from the 15th century), sanguinarius bloodthirsty (sanguinary), con-sanguineus of one blood — kin (consanguinity), ex-sanguis bloodless (exsanguinate), Spanish sangre, Italian sangue, French sang (sang-froid, cold blood — composure), sanguinolent bloodstained, sangria Spanish \"bleeding\" — blood-red wine."],
  pre: ["prae(在前)来自印欧语 *preh₂i(在前),同根有 pro、per、prior、primus、英语 for、fore、first,希腊语 para。罗马人的 prae- 表示时间和空间的\"在前\":prae-dicere 预言,prae-videre 预见,prae-cedere 走在前(precede),prae-sidere 坐在前面——主持(preside、president 是主持者),prae-fectus 被放在前面的——长官(prefect),prae-textus 借口,prae-mium 先拿的(premium),prae-iudicium 偏见,也表示\"极度\":prae-clarus 极著名,prae-potens 极强。法语把它变成 pré-、英语 pre-;preface 是 prae-fatio 前言,prevent 是 prae-venire\"走在前面\"——先是\"预先\"后是\"阻止\"。",
    "prae (before) comes from PIE *preh₂i (in front), shared with pro, per, prior, primus, English for, fore, first, Greek para. Roman prae- marks \"before\" in time and place: prae-dicere to foretell, prae-videre to foresee, prae-cedere to go before (precede), prae-sidere to sit in front — to preside (a president presides), prae-fectus set in front — a prefect, prae-textus a pretext, prae-mium what is taken first (premium), prae-iudicium prejudice; it also intensifies: prae-clarus very famous, prae-potens very powerful. French made it pré-, English pre-; a preface is prae-fatio, words before, prevent is prae-venire, to come before — first to anticipate, then to stop."],
  ord: ["ordo(行列、秩序,属格 ordinis)可能来自织布:ordiri 是\"起经线\"(与希腊语 ordos 无关?存疑)——织机上排好的线就是 ordo;同根有 primordium(最初的经线——开端)、exordium(织物的开头——序言)。罗马的 ordo 是社会等级:ordo senatorius、ordo equester(元老、骑士阶层);ordinare 排列、任命(ordain 是授圣职,ordinance 法令,ordinary 是\"按序的\"——普通,extraordinary 序外的),ordinatio 排序,sub-ordinare 排在下面(subordinate),co-ordinare 排在一起(coordinate 1640 年代),ordinal 序数,order 经法语 ordre(命令义 13 世纪,修会 order 是 ordo 的中世纪义),orderly 有序。",
    "ordo (a row, order, genitive ordinis) probably comes from weaving: ordiri is to set up the warp — the threads ranged on the loom were an ordo; cognates are primordium (the first warp — a beginning) and exordium (the start of the web — a preface). Roman ordo was social rank: the ordo senatorius and ordo equester; ordinare to arrange, to appoint (ordain, of priests; ordinance a decree; ordinary \"in due order\" — usual, extraordinary out of order), ordinatio ordering, sub-ordinare to rank below (subordinate), co-ordinare to rank together (coordinate, 1640s), ordinal, order through French ordre (the command sense from the 13th century; a religious order is medieval ordo), orderly."],
  plen: ["plenus(满)来自印欧语 *pleh₁-(充满),同根有希腊语 plērēs(满)、polys(多——poly-)、英语 full、fill、德语 voll、梵语 pūrṇa。罗马人 plenus 是装满的;plenitudo 丰满(plenitude),plenarius 全体的(plenary 会议),plenum 物理学的\"充满空间\"(与 vacuum 相对),com-plere 填满(complete、complement 补足、compliment 是西班牙语 cumplimiento\"礼数周全\"——同一个词的两种拼法!),im-plere 装满(implement 是\"填满\"用的工具,supplement 补上),re-plere 再装(replete 饱的,replenish),ex-plere 填足(expletive 是填空的词——脏话),plus 更多(比较级 pleior 的缩合),plebs 平民是\"众多的人\",plenty 是 plenitas 经法语。",
    "plenus (full) comes from PIE *pleh₁- (to fill), shared with Greek plērēs (full), polys (many — poly-), English full and fill, German voll, Sanskrit pūrṇa. Roman plenus is filled; plenitudo fullness (plenitude), plenarius of the whole body (a plenary session), plenum physics' filled space (against the vacuum), com-plere to fill up (complete; complement makes full, and compliment is Spanish cumplimiento, fulfilling courtesy — the same word twice spelt!), im-plere to fill in (an implement fills a need, a supplement fills up), re-plere to fill again (replete, replenish), ex-plere to fill out (an expletive fills a gap — hence swear-words), plus more (comparative pleior contracted), plebs the many, plenty plenitas through French."],
  mut: ["mutare(变、换)来自印欧语 *mei-(换、变),同根有 munus(交换的礼物)、communis、英语 mean(共同的)、哥特语 maidjan(变)。罗马人 mutare 换衣、换地方、变心;mutatio 变化(mutation 是德弗里斯 1901 年的遗传学术语),mutabilis 易变(mutable、immutable),com-mutare 交换(commute 本义\"以一换一\"——减刑是换刑,通勤是 19 世纪美国的\"换购月票\"),per-mutare 置换(permutation),trans-mutare 转变(炼金术的 transmutation),mutuus 是互换的——相互(mutual;mutuum 借贷),moult 换毛是 mutare 经古英语 mūtian,mew(鹰笼)是换羽的地方。",
    "mutare (to change, exchange) comes from PIE *mei- (to exchange, change), shared with munus (a gift exchanged), communis, English mean (common), Gothic maidjan (to alter). Romans mutare clothes, places, minds; mutatio a change (mutation is De Vries' 1901 genetic term), mutabilis changeable (mutable, immutable), com-mutare to exchange (commute meant one-for-one exchange — a sentence commuted is swapped for a lighter one; commuting to work is 19th-century American, from \"commuting\" a season ticket), per-mutare to interchange (permutation), trans-mutare to transform (the alchemists' transmutation), mutuus exchanged — mutual (a mutuum a loan), moult is mutare through Old English mūtian, a mew is where hawks moulted."],
  rap: ["rapere(抓走、抢)来自印欧语 *rep-(抓、扯),同根有希腊语 ereptomai(撕食)、立陶宛语 rėpti(抓)。罗马神话的\"劫掠萨宾妇女\"是 raptus Sabinarum——rapere 先指强行掳走(rapt、rapture 是被\"抓到天上去\"的狂喜,raptor 猛禽是抓的鸟),rapidus 是抓得快——湍急、迅速(rapid),rapina 抢劫(rapine、ravine 是被急流\"抢\"出的深沟),rapax 贪婪(rapacious),sur-ripere 偷(surreptitious 偷偷的),cor-ripere 抓住责备(correptio),e-ripere 夺走,ravish 是 rapere 经法语 ravir——先指掳走后指陶醉,ravage 蹂躏,rape 法律义 15 世纪固定。",
    "rapere (to seize, carry off) comes from PIE *rep- (to snatch), shared with Greek ereptomai (to tear at) and Lithuanian rėpti (to grasp). Rome's Rape of the Sabine Women is the raptus Sabinarum — rapere meant abduction (rapt and rapture are being snatched up to heaven, a raptor a snatching bird), rapidus snatching fast — swift (rapid), rapina plunder (rapine; a ravine is a gorge \"snatched\" out by torrent), rapax greedy (rapacious), sur-ripere to steal (surreptitious), cor-ripere to seize and rebuke, e-ripere to snatch away, ravish is rapere through French ravir — to carry off, then to enrapture, ravage, and rape in its legal sense settled in the 15th century."],
  od: ["希腊语 aoidē/ōidē(歌)来自 aeidein(唱),印欧语 *h₂weid-(唱、说)存疑。ōidē 是抒情诗——品达的胜利颂;trag-ōidia 是\"山羊之歌\"(狄俄尼索斯祭典上的合唱,或以山羊为奖),kōm-ōidia 是\"狂欢队之歌\"(comedy),mel-ōidia 曲调之歌(melody),rhaps-ōidia 是\"缝起来的歌\"——吟游诗人把史诗段落缝合(rhapsody),par-ōidia 仿歌(parody),pros-ōidia 加在歌上的——声调、韵律(prosody),palin-ōidia 收回前言的歌(palinode),ode 是 1580 年代英语借的,epode 后歌,monody 独唱。",
    "Greek aoidē/ōidē (a song) comes from aeidein (to sing), possibly PIE *h₂weid- (to sing, speak). An ōidē is a lyric poem — Pindar's victory odes; trag-ōidia is the goat-song (a chorus at Dionysus' festival, or a goat as prize), kōm-ōidia the revel-song (comedy), mel-ōidia tune-song (melody), rhaps-ōidia the stitched song — bards sewing epic passages together (rhapsody), par-ōidia a song alongside (parody), pros-ōidia what is added to the song — accent, metre (prosody), palin-ōidia a song taking back what was said (palinode); ode entered English in the 1580s, epode an after-song, monody a solo lament."],
  hered: ["heres(继承人,属格 heredis)来自印欧语 *ǵʰeh₁-(空缺、离开)加后缀——继承人是\"填补空缺的人\";同根有希腊语 khēros(丧失的)、khēra(寡妇)、梵语 hā(离开)。罗马法的继承是\"接续人格\":heres 承担死者的一切债务与祭祀;hereditas 遗产(heredity 到 19 世纪才转为生物遗传,1863 年斯宾塞),hereditarius 世袭的(hereditary),heir 经法语 heir,heirloom 是继承的\"工具\"(loom 古义器具),inherit 是 in-hereditare,heritage 遗产,disinherit 剥夺继承权。",
    "heres (an heir, genitive heredis) comes from PIE *ǵʰeh₁- (to be empty, to leave) with a suffix — the heir fills the gap left; cognates are Greek khēros (bereft), khēra (widow), Sanskrit hā (to abandon). Roman inheritance was continuation of the person: the heres took on the dead man's debts and rites; hereditas the estate (heredity became biological only in the 19th century, Spencer 1863), hereditarius hereditary, heir through French heir, an heirloom an inherited implement (loom once meant a tool), inherit is in-hereditare, heritage, disinherit."],
  oct: ["octo(八)是印欧语 *oḱtōu,希腊语 oktō、梵语 aṣṭa、英语 eight、德语 acht、哥特语 ahtau。罗马旧历八月叫 October(第八月),改历后没改名;octavus 第八(octave 是八度音——第八个音,也指节日后第八天),octo-genarius 八十岁,octopus 是希腊语\"八足\",oct-ans 八分仪,octet 八重奏,octagon 八边形,octane 辛烷(八个碳),octavo 是全张纸对折三次的开本(八开)。Octavianus 屋大维的名字来自 Octavius 氏族——\"第八\"。",
    "octo (eight) is Indo-European *oḱtōu: Greek oktō, Sanskrit aṣṭa, English eight, German acht, Gothic ahtau. In the old Roman calendar October was the eighth month, and kept its name after the reform; octavus eighth (an octave is the eighth note, and the eighth day after a feast), octo-genarius eighty years old, octopus Greek eight-foot, an octant an eighth of a circle, octet, octagon, octane (eight carbons), octavo a sheet folded three times into eight leaves. Octavian's name came from the gens Octavia — \"eighth\"."],
  rud: ["rudis(粗糙的、未加工的)可能来自印欧语 *reu-(挖、扯)存疑。罗马人的 rudis 是未经打磨的原料,也是角斗士退役时得到的木剑(rudis)——象征\"粗木\";rudimentum 是初学、雏形(rudiment),e-rudire 是\"去掉粗糙\"——教育(erudite 有学问的),rudis 的中性 rude 经法语——粗鲁(13 世纪),rudeness,crude 与此无关(crudus 生的)。",
    "rudis (rough, unworked) may come from PIE *reu- (to dig, tear). Roman rudis was raw material, and also the wooden sword given a gladiator on retirement — the \"rough stick\" of freedom; rudimentum a first attempt, a beginning (rudiment), e-rudire to take the roughness off — to educate (erudite), the neuter rude through French — rough-mannered (13th century), rudeness; crude is unrelated (crudus, raw)."],
  suad: ["suadere(劝)与 suavis(甜)同根:印欧语 *sweh₂d-(甜),同根有希腊语 hēdys(甜——hedonism)、英语 sweet、德语 süß、梵语 svādu。劝说就是\"把事情说得甜\";per-suadere 彻底劝动(persuade、persuasion),dis-suadere 劝阻(dissuade),suasio 建议,suavis 甜美的(suave 是 19 世纪的\"圆滑\"),suavitas 温和,assuage 是 ad-suaviare 使甜——缓和,sweet 与 suave 是同一个甜。",
    "suadere (to advise) shares a root with suavis (sweet): PIE *sweh₂d- (sweet), shared with Greek hēdys (sweet — hedonism), English sweet, German süß, Sanskrit svādu. To persuade is to make a thing sweet; per-suadere to advise thoroughly (persuade, persuasion), dis-suadere to advise against (dissuade), suasio counsel, suavis sweet (suave in the 19th century came to mean smoothly polite), suavitas mildness, assuage is ad-suaviare, to sweeten — to soften; sweet and suave are one sweetness."],
  tot: ["totus(整个)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能来自 *touto-(部落、全体,与凯尔特语 tuath、日耳曼语 þeudō——Deutsch 德意志\"人民的\"同根)。罗马人 totus 是全部;totalis 是中世纪拉丁语(total 14 世纪),totalitas 总体,in toto 整体地,totus 的法语形状 tout(tout le monde 所有人),tutto 意大利语,todo 西班牙语,totalitarian 是 1920 年代意大利法西斯的自称 totalitario,teetotal 与此无关(t-total 强调的 total),tote 与此无关。",
    "totus (whole) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin — perhaps *touto- (the tribe, the whole people, kin to Celtic tuath and Germanic þeudō, whence Deutsch, \"of the people\"). Roman totus is all of a thing; totalis is medieval Latin (total, 14th century), totalitas the whole, in toto entirely, the French form tout (tout le monde, everyone), Italian tutto, Spanish todo, totalitarian was Italian Fascism's own word totalitario in the 1920s; teetotal (t-total, an emphatic total) and tote are unrelated."],
  meta: ["希腊语 meta(在中间、之后、变化)来自印欧语 *me-(在中间),同根有拉丁语 无对应、英语 mid、德语 mit(与)。meta-morphōsis 形变,meta-phora 搬过去——比喻(metaphor),meta-bolē 变化(metabolism 1878),meth-odos 顺路,meta-physika 是安德罗尼库斯编亚里士多德文集时\"排在《物理学》之后\"的那些卷——形而上学(metaphysics 是书架顺序变成的哲学名),meta-thesis 换位,meta-stasis 转移(癌),meta-data 关于数据的数据(1968),meta- 表示\"关于自身\"是 20 世纪的用法(meta-language 1936)。",
    "Greek meta (among, after, change) comes from PIE *me- (in the middle), shared with English mid and German mit (with). meta-morphōsis a change of shape, meta-phora a carrying over — metaphor, meta-bolē a change (metabolism 1878), meth-odos the way along, meta-physika the books Andronicus shelved \"after the Physics\" in Aristotle's works — metaphysics is a shelf-order turned into a branch of philosophy, meta-thesis transposition, meta-stasis a change of place (of cancer), meta-data data about data (1968); meta- meaning \"about itself\" is 20th-century (meta-language 1936)."],
  stru: ["struere(堆、建)来自印欧语 *ster-(铺开),同根有 sternere(铺)、stratum(层——street 是 via strata 铺过的路)、英语 strew、straw(铺的草)、德语 streuen。罗马人 struere 堆柴、列阵、建造;structura 建造、结构(structure),con-struere 一起堆(construct),in-struere 在里面堆——装备、教导(instruct;instrument 是\"用来装备的\",instruction 指令),de-struere 拆掉(destroy、destruction),ob-struere 堆在前面(obstruct),industria 是 indu-struere\"往里堆\"——勤勉(industry 的工业义 18 世纪),strues 柴堆。",
    "struere (to heap, build) comes from PIE *ster- (to spread), shared with sternere (to strew), stratum (a layer — a street is a via strata, a paved road), English strew and straw (what is strewn), German streuen. Romans struere piled wood, drew up lines, built; structura a building, a structure, con-struere to pile together (construct), in-struere to pile within — to equip, to teach (instruct; an instrument is equipment, an instruction a direction), de-struere to unbuild (destroy, destruction), ob-struere to pile in the way (obstruct), industria is indu-struere, building within — diligence (industry's factory sense is 18th-century), strues a pile."],
  path: ["希腊语 pathos(遭受、感受)来自 paskhein(遭受),印欧语 *kʷenth-(受苦)。亚里士多德的 pathos 是修辞里打动听众的情感(与 logos、ethos 并列);pathē 是灵魂被动的感受——passion 是拉丁语 passio 的直译(passio 是基督的受难),path-ologia 是\"病之学\"(pathology 1610 年代),sym-patheia 同感(sympathy),em-patheia 是 1909 年铁钦纳为译德语 Einfühlung 造的 empathy,anti-pathēs 反感,a-patheia 是斯多葛派的\"不动心\"(apathy 后贬为冷漠),tele-pathy 1882 年迈尔斯造,psycho-path 1885,-pathy 疗法(homeo-pathy 1826),pathetic 先指\"感人的\",19 世纪转为\"可怜的\"。",
    "Greek pathos (suffering, feeling) comes from paskhein (to undergo), PIE *kʷenth- (to suffer). Aristotle's pathos is the emotion that moves an audience (beside logos and ethos); the pathē are the soul's passive states — passion is Latin passio, their translation (the passio is Christ's suffering), path-ologia the study of disease (pathology 1610s), sym-patheia feeling with (sympathy), em-patheia Titchener's 1909 coinage for German Einfühlung (empathy), anti-pathēs feeling against, a-patheia the Stoic's freedom from passion (apathy later sank to indifference), tele-pathy Myers 1882, psycho-path 1885, -pathy a treatment (homeo-pathy 1826), pathetic meant moving before the 19th century made it pitiable."],
  pan: ["panis(面包)来自印欧语 *peh₂-(喂养、保护),同根有 pascere(放牧——pastor 牧人、pasture)、pabulum(饲料)、pater(?)存疑、英语 food、feed、fodder。罗马人的 panis 是主食;\"面包与竞技\"(panem et circenses)是尤维纳利斯对民众的讥讽;panarium 面包篮(pantry 是 paneterie 放面包的房间),com-panio 是\"一起吃面包的人\"——同伴(company 公司本义一群同伴,companion),ap-paner 给面包——appanage 是给王子的封地,pane 意大利语、pain 法语、pan 西班牙语,empanada 包在面团里的,panettone 大面包,pannier 面包篮——驮篮。",
    "panis (bread) comes from PIE *peh₂- (to feed, protect), shared with pascere (to pasture — pastor, pasture), pabulum (fodder), English food, feed, fodder. Roman panis was the staple; \"bread and circuses\" (panem et circenses) is Juvenal's sneer at the populace; a panarium a bread-basket (pantry is paneterie, the bread room), a com-panio one who shares bread — a companion (a company was a band of companions), ap-paner to give bread — an appanage is a prince's provision, Italian pane, French pain, Spanish pan, empanada wrapped in dough, panettone a big loaf, pannier a bread-basket — a pack-basket."],
  dorm: ["dormire(睡)来自印欧语 *drem-(睡),同根有希腊语 darthanein(睡)、梵语 drāti、俄语 dremat'(打盹)、古教会斯拉夫语 dremati。罗马人 dormire 睡觉,dormitorium 宿舍(dormitory;dorm 美式缩写 1900 年),dormitio 安眠——圣母安息(Dormition),ob-dormire 睡去,dormant 法语\"睡着的\"——休眠(火山、账户),dormouse 睡鼠是法语 dormeuse(爱睡的),dormer 屋顶窗是 dormitorium 的窗,dortoir 法语宿舍,dormir 法语/西班牙语。",
    "dormire (to sleep) comes from PIE *drem- (to sleep), shared with Greek darthanein (to sleep), Sanskrit drāti, Russian dremat' (to doze), Old Church Slavonic dremati. Romans dormire to sleep; dormitorium a sleeping-room (dormitory; dorm is American clipping, 1900), dormitio falling asleep — the Dormition of the Virgin, ob-dormire to fall asleep, dormant French \"sleeping\" — of volcanoes and accounts, dormouse French dormeuse, the sleepy one, a dormer a window of the sleeping-room, French dortoir a dormitory, dormir French and Spanish."],
  fug: ["fugere(逃)来自印欧语 *bʰeug-(逃),同根有希腊语 pheugein(逃——phygē 流放)、立陶宛语 būgti(受惊)。罗马人 fugere 逃跑,fuga 逃亡(fugue 是意大利语 fuga——赋格曲里声部\"追逐\",1590 年代),fugitivus 逃奴(fugitive),re-fugium 避难所(refuge、refugee 是 1685 年逃出法国的胡格诺派),sub-terfugium 遁词(subterfuge),centri-fugus 离心的(1720 年代),tempus fugit 时光飞逝(维吉尔),fugacious 易逝,febri-fuge 退烧药,vermi-fuge 驱虫药。",
    "fugere (to flee) comes from PIE *bʰeug- (to flee), shared with Greek pheugein (to flee — phygē, exile), Lithuanian būgti (to take fright). Romans fugere to run away; fuga flight (a fugue is Italian fuga — the voices chase each other, 1590s), fugitivus a runaway slave (fugitive), re-fugium a place fled to (refuge; the refugees were the Huguenots who fled France in 1685), sub-terfugium an evasion (subterfuge), centri-fugus fleeing the centre (1720s), tempus fugit time flies (Virgil), fugacious fleeting, a febrifuge puts fever to flight, a vermifuge worms."],
  acr: ["acer(尖、锐,属格 acris)与 acus(针)、acies(刃、战线)、acidus(酸)、希腊语 akros(顶端、尖)、akmē(顶点)同根:印欧语 *h₂eḱ-(尖),同根的英语词有 edge、ear(麦穗)、awn。罗马人 acer 是味道的辛辣和性情的锐利;acerbus 苦涩(acerbic),acri-monia 尖刻(acrimony),acutus 磨尖的(acute 急性、尖锐;cute 是 acute 的缩写!),ex-acerbare 加剧(exacerbate),acumen 尖端——敏锐,acid 酸(1620 年代),acetum 醋(acetic),acupuncture 针刺(1680 年代),希腊线:acro-polis 高城,acro-bat 尖端上走的人,acro-nym 首字母词(1943),acme 顶点,acne 是 akmē 的抄写错误!",
    "acer (sharp, genitive acris) belongs with acus (a needle), acies (an edge, a battle-line), acidus (sour), Greek akros (topmost, pointed), akmē (a peak): PIE *h₂eḱ- (sharp), the root of English edge, ear (of corn), awn. Roman acer was sharpness of taste and of temper; acerbus bitter (acerbic), acri-monia sharpness (acrimony), acutus sharpened (acute; cute is acute clipped!), ex-acerbare to sharpen further (exacerbate), acumen a point — keenness, acid (1620s), acetum vinegar (acetic), acupuncture needle-pricking (1680s); the Greek line: acro-polis high city, acro-bat walker on tiptoe, acro-nym a tip-name (1943), acme the peak, and acne is a scribal error for akmē!"],
  centr: ["希腊语 kentron(刺、圆规的针脚、中心)来自 kentein(刺),印欧语 *kent-(刺)。画圆时圆规的针脚就是 kentron——中心;罗马人借为 centrum,centralis 中心的(central),con-centrare 聚到中心(concentrate 1640 年代),ex-centricus 离开中心的(eccentric 先是天文学的偏心轨道,后指怪人),centri-fugus 离心,centri-petus 向心(牛顿 1687),centre 经法语,epicentre 震中(1880 年代),concentric 同心,egocentric 以自我为中心(1900),decentralize 分权,centrist 中间派。",
    "Greek kentron (a goad, the point of a compass, a centre) comes from kentein (to prick), PIE *kent- (to prick). The compass point that stays still while the circle is drawn is the kentron — the centre; Rome borrowed it as centrum, centralis central, con-centrare to bring to the centre (concentrate, 1640s), ex-centricus off-centre (eccentric was an astronomer's orbit before it was an odd person), centri-fugus centre-fleeing, centri-petus centre-seeking (Newton 1687), centre through French, epicentre (1880s), concentric, egocentric (1900), decentralize, centrist."],
  arc: ["arcus(弓、拱)可能来自印欧语 *h₂erkʷo-(弓、箭),同根有哥特语 arhwazna(箭)、古英语 earh(箭——arrow 经古挪威语)。罗马人的 arcus 是弓也是拱门(arcus triumphalis 凯旋门);arcuatus 弓形的(arcuate),arcaria 拱廊(arcade 经意大利语 arcata),arc 弧,arch 经法语 arche,archer 弓箭手,arciform 弓形,arbalest 是 arcu-ballista 弩,arcade 游戏厅是从拱廊里的娱乐场来的(1930 年代)。archi-(首要)是另一个词(希腊语 arkhein)。",
    "arcus (a bow, an arch) may come from PIE *h₂erkʷo- (bow, arrow), shared with Gothic arhwazna (arrow) and Old English earh (arrow — arrow itself came via Old Norse). Roman arcus was the archer's bow and the arch (the arcus triumphalis, triumphal arch); arcuatus bow-shaped (arcuate), arcaria an arched walk (arcade through Italian arcata), arc, arch through French arche, archer, arciform, an arbalest an arcu-ballista, a crossbow, the amusement arcade named from games set up under arcades (1930s). archi- (chief) is another word (Greek arkhein)."],
  cel: ["celare(藏)来自印欧语 *ḱel-(遮、藏),同根有英语 hell(藏起来的地方)、hall(遮蔽处)、hull(壳)、helmet(遮头)、德语 hehlen(窝藏)、Hölle、希腊语 kalyptein(遮——apocalypse 是揭开)、kalyx(花萼)。罗马人 celare 隐藏,cella 是储藏室——小房间(cell:修士的小室、牢房、细胞——胡克 1665 年看到软木的\"小室\"),cellarium 地窖(cellar),con-celare 完全藏起(conceal),oc-cultus 是 ob-celare 的分词——被盖住的(occult 神秘学是\"隐藏的学问\"),clam 秘密地,cellulose 细胞质(1839),color(颜色)也来自这个根——\"遮盖物\"。",
    "celare (to hide) comes from PIE *ḱel- (to cover, conceal), shared with English hell (the hidden place), hall (a shelter), hull, helmet, German hehlen (to harbour), Hölle, Greek kalyptein (to cover — an apocalypse is an uncovering), kalyx (a cup). Romans celare to hide; cella a storeroom — a small room (cell: a monk's, a prisoner's, and Hooke's 1665 name for the little chambers he saw in cork), cellarium a cellar, con-celare to hide completely (conceal), oc-cultus the participle of ob-celare — covered over (the occult is hidden knowledge), clam secretly, cellulose (1839); color, colour, also comes from this root — a covering."],
  cycl: ["希腊语 kyklos(圆、轮)来自印欧语 *kʷekʷlo-(轮),是 *kʷel-(转)的重叠形式,同根有英语 wheel、梵语 cakra(轮——脉轮)、托卡里安语 kukäl(车)。希腊人的 kyklos 是圆、轮、循环——kyklikoi 是荷马之后写\"史诗圈\"的诗人;cyclus 拉丁语借入,cycle 循环(14 世纪),bi-cycle 是 1868 年法语的\"两轮\",en-kyklios 圆圈里的——encyclopaedia 全套教育,cyclone 是皮丁顿 1848 年造的\"盘旋风暴\",Cyclops 圆眼,cyclotron 1935,recycle 1926,cyclical 周期的,motorcycle 1894。",
    "Greek kyklos (a circle, a wheel) comes from PIE *kʷekʷlo- (wheel), a reduplicated form of *kʷel- (to turn), shared with English wheel, Sanskrit cakra (a wheel — the chakras), Tocharian kukäl (a cart). Greek kyklos was a circle, a wheel, a cycle — the kyklikoi were the poets who filled in the epic cycle after Homer; cyclus was borrowed into Latin, cycle (14th century), bi-cycle French \"two-wheel\" of 1868, en-kyklios within the circle — encyclopaedia, cyclone Piddington's 1848 \"whirling storm\", Cyclops round-eye, cyclotron 1935, recycle 1926, cyclical, motorcycle 1894."],
  mon: ["monere(提醒、警告)来自印欧语 *men-(想),同根有 mens(心智)、memini(记得)、希腊语 mnēmē(记忆)、英语 mind、德语 mahnen(告诫)。它是\"让人想起\"的使役式;monitor 是提醒者——学校里的班长、后指监视器(1930 年代),monitio 警告,ad-monere 告诫(admonish),prae-monere 预警(premonition),monumentum 是\"让人记住的东西\"——纪念碑(monument),monstrum 是神的警示——异象、怪物(monster;demonstrate 是指给人看,monstrance 圣体显供架),summon 是 sub-monere 悄悄提醒——传唤(经法语),money 是 Juno Moneta(\"警示者\"朱诺,罗马造币厂设在她的神庙里),mint 造币厂也是 moneta。",
    "monere (to remind, warn) comes from PIE *men- (to think), shared with mens (mind), memini (I remember), Greek mnēmē (memory), English mind, German mahnen (to admonish). It is the causative: to make someone think; a monitor is a reminder — a school prefect, later a watching screen (1930s), monitio a warning, ad-monere to admonish, prae-monere to forewarn (premonition), monumentum what makes you remember — a monument, monstrum a divine warning — a portent, a monster (demonstrate is to show, a monstrance shows the host), summon is sub-monere, to remind privately — to call to court (through French), money is Juno Moneta, Juno the Warner, in whose temple Rome's mint stood — and mint is moneta too."],
  idio: ["希腊语 idios(自己的、私人的)来自印欧语 *swe-(自己),与拉丁语 suus、se 同根(希腊语把 sw- 变成了 h-/i-)。idiōtēs 是\"私人\"——不参与公共事务的普通人,后指外行,再贬为傻子(idiot 经拉丁语 idiota);idiōma 是\"独有的说法\"(idiom 习语,西班牙语 idioma 是语言),idio-synkrasia 是\"自己特有的混合\"(idiosyncrasy,原是医学上的个人体质),idio-pathic 特发的(原因不明),idiolect 个人语言(1948),idiotic。雅典人看不起 idiōtēs——不问政治的人。",
    "Greek idios (one's own, private) comes from PIE *swe- (self), kin to Latin suus and se (Greek turned sw- into h-/i-). An idiōtēs was a private person — one who took no part in public life, then a layman, then a fool (idiot via Latin idiota); an idiōma a private way of speaking (idiom; Spanish idioma is a language), idio-synkrasia one's own peculiar mixture (idiosyncrasy, originally a patient's individual constitution), idio-pathic arising of itself (of unknown cause), idiolect a personal language (1948), idiotic. Athenians despised the idiōtēs — the man who ignored politics."],
  ambul: ["ambulare(走)是 amb-(绕)+ *el-(走,与希腊语 elaunein 驱赶、alaomai 游荡同根)。罗马人 ambulare 是散步,ambulatio 是散步廊;praeambulum 是\"走在前面的\"——序言(preamble),ambulantia 是 19 世纪法语 hôpital ambulant(随军移动的医院)——救护车(ambulance,1854 年克里米亚战争),ambulatory 能走的、回廊,somn-ambulus 梦游者,per-ambulare 走一遍(perambulator 婴儿车——\"走遍\"的车,pram 是它的缩写),amble 溜达(经法语 ambler),alley 是法语 allée(走道),funambulist 走绳索的。",
    "ambulare (to walk) is amb- (around) + *el- (to go, kin to Greek elaunein, to drive, and alaomai, to wander). Romans ambulare to stroll; an ambulatio was a covered walk; praeambulum \"what walks in front\" — a preamble, ambulantia was 19th-century French hôpital ambulant, the field hospital that moved with the army — an ambulance (Crimea, 1854), ambulatory able to walk, and a cloister walk, somn-ambulus a sleepwalker, per-ambulare to walk through (a perambulator is the walk-about carriage — pram is its clipping), amble through French ambler, alley French allée, a walk, a funambulist walks the rope."],
  apt: ["aptus(合适的、系好的)是 apere(系上)的分词,印欧语 *h₂ep-(抓、系),同根有梵语 āpnoti(达到)、赫梯语 epzi(抓)。罗马人 aptus 是装配好的、合适的;aptitudo 适合(aptitude 天资),ad-aptare 使适合(adapt),in-eptus 不合适的——愚蠢(inept),co-aptare 接合,apt 恰当,attitude 是 aptitudo 经意大利语 attitudine(画家说的人物\"姿态\"——1660 年代,19 世纪转为心态),adept 是 adeptus\"已达到的\"——炼金术里得到秘方的人,copula 系词也是 co-apula(连接)。",
    "aptus (fitted, suitable) is the participle of apere (to fasten), PIE *h₂ep- (to grasp, fasten), shared with Sanskrit āpnoti (reaches) and Hittite epzi (seizes). Roman aptus is well-joined, fitting; aptitudo fitness (aptitude), ad-aptare to fit to (adapt), in-eptus unfitting — foolish (inept), co-aptare to join, apt fitting, attitude is aptitudo through Italian attitudine — a painter's word for a figure's posture (1660s), a state of mind from the 19th century, adept is adeptus, \"having attained\" — the alchemist who had found the secret, and the copula is co-apula, a joining."],
  integr: ["integer(完整的、未触的)是 in-(不)+ tag-(触,tangere 的词根):\"没被碰过的\"。罗马人的 integer 是完好、清白;integritas 完整、正直(integrity 两个义都还在),integrare 恢复完整(integrate 1630 年代,社会义 1940 年代),re-integrare 重新恢复,dis-integrare 解体,integer 数学上的整数(1570 年代),entire 是 integer 经法语 entier,integral 整体的——积分(莱布尼茨 1690),integument 是 tegere 的(盖),intact 也是 in-tactus 未触的。",
    "integer (whole, untouched) is in- (not) + tag- (the root of tangere, to touch): \"not touched\". Roman integer is unharmed, upright; integritas wholeness and honesty (integrity keeps both), integrare to make whole (integrate, 1630s; the social sense from the 1940s), re-integrare to restore, dis-integrare to fall apart, integer the whole number (1570s), entire is integer through French entier, integral of the whole — the integral calculus (Leibniz 1690); integument is from tegere (to cover), and intact is likewise in-tactus, untouched."],
  priv: ["privus(单个的、自己的)来自印欧语 *prei-wo-(在前的、分开的),与 pro、prae 同根,也与 proprius 有关。罗马人 privatus 是\"退出公共生活的\"——私人(private;privatus 是与 publicus 相对的),privare 是\"使脱离\"——剥夺(deprive、privation 匮乏),privi-legium 是\"为个人立的法\"——特权(privilege,《十二表法》禁止 privilegia),privacy 1590 年代,privy 是私密的(枢密院 Privy Council;privy 厕所),privateer 私掠船(1660 年代),privet 女贞与此无关。",
    "privus (single, one's own) comes from PIE *prei-wo- (in front, apart), kin to pro and prae and related to proprius. Roman privatus was \"withdrawn from public life\" — private, the opposite of publicus; privare is to set apart from — to deprive (privation), privi-legium a law for one individual — a privilege (the Twelve Tables forbade privilegia), privacy 1590s, privy secret (the Privy Council; a privy the private room), a privateer a private warship (1660s); privet the hedge is unrelated."],
  pur: ["purus(纯净的)来自印欧语 *peuH-(净化),同根有梵语 pavate(净化)、punāti、希腊语 pyr(火——净化之火,pyre、pyro-)存疑。罗马人 purus 是干净、无杂质的;puritas 纯度(purity),purgare 是 purum agere 使纯——清洗(purge、purgatory 炼狱是 12 世纪的神学地名),ex-purgare 删净(expurgate),purificare 净化,Puritan 是 1560 年代的讥称,pure 经法语 pur,puree 是法语\"过筛净化\"的菜泥,purge 泻药,Purim 与此无关(希伯来语)。",
    "purus (clean, pure) comes from PIE *peuH- (to purify), shared with Sanskrit pavate (purifies), punāti, and perhaps Greek pyr (fire, the purifier — pyre, pyro-). Roman purus is clean, unmixed; puritas purity, purgare is purum agere, to make pure — to purge (purgatory is a 12th-century theological place-name), ex-purgare to cleanse a text (expurgate), purificare to purify, Puritan a 1560s taunt, pure through French pur, a purée French for sieved-clean, a purge a laxative; Purim is unrelated (Hebrew)."],
  somn: ["somnus(睡)来自印欧语 *swep-no-(睡),同根有希腊语 hypnos(hypnosis)、梵语 svapna(梦)、英语 sweven(古义\"梦\")、拉丁语 sopor(昏睡——soporific)、sopire(使睡)。罗马的 Somnus 是睡神,儿子是 Morpheus;somnium 梦,somni-fer 催眠的,somnolentus 困倦(somnolent),in-somnia 无眠(insomnia),somn-ambulus 梦游者,somniloquy 说梦话,sopor 深睡(soporific 是 1690 年代),hypnotic 希腊线。",
    "somnus (sleep) comes from PIE *swep-no- (sleep), shared with Greek hypnos (hypnosis), Sanskrit svapna (dream), English sweven (an old word for dream), Latin sopor (deep sleep — soporific), sopire (to put to sleep). Roman Somnus was the god of sleep and Morpheus his son; somnium a dream, somni-fer sleep-bringing, somnolentus drowsy (somnolent), in-somnia sleeplessness, somn-ambulus a sleepwalker, somniloquy talking in sleep, sopor stupor (soporific 1690s), hypnotic from the Greek line."],
  ir: ["ira(怒)来自印欧语 *h₁eis-(冲动、激情),同根有希腊语 hieros(神圣的——受神灵鼓动的)、oistros(牛虻、狂热——oestrus 发情),梵语 iṣirá(有力的)、阿维斯陀语 aēšma(愤怒——魔王 Aeshma Daeva 即《托比传》的 Asmodeus)。罗马人 ira 是怒火,iratus 发怒的(irate),irasci 发怒,iracundus 易怒的(irascible),ire 经法语,de-liria 与此无关(lira 犁沟),斯多葛派塞涅卡写了《论愤怒》(De Ira)。",
    "ira (anger) comes from PIE *h₁eis- (impulse, passion), shared with Greek hieros (holy — moved by the god), oistros (gadfly, frenzy — oestrus), Sanskrit iṣirá (vigorous), Avestan aēšma (wrath — the demon Aeshma Daeva, the Asmodeus of Tobit). Roman ira is rage, iratus angered (irate), irasci to grow angry, iracundus quick-tempered (irascible), ire through French; Seneca the Stoic wrote a treatise De Ira, On Anger."],
  nerv: ["nervus(筋、腱、弓弦)来自印欧语 *(s)neh₁ur-(筋、腱),同根有希腊语 neuron(筋——neuro-,neurology 1680 年代)、梵语 snāvan、亚美尼亚语 neard、英语 sinew(经 *snēwō)。罗马人的 nervus 是腱、弓弦、琴弦——也是\"力量\"(nervi belli pecunia,金钱是战争的筋腱);解剖学 16 世纪后把 nervus 专用于神经;nervosus 多筋的——19 世纪转为神经质,e-nervare 抽掉筋——使无力(enervate),nerve 从筋(14 世纪)到神经(17 世纪)到胆量(19 世纪),nervy,neurosis 1777,neuron 1884。",
    "nervus (sinew, tendon, bowstring) comes from PIE *(s)neh₁ur- (sinew), shared with Greek neuron (sinew — neuro-, neurology 1680s), Sanskrit snāvan, Armenian neard, English sinew (via *snēwō). Roman nervus was tendon, bowstring, lyre-string — and strength (nervi belli pecunia, money is the sinews of war); anatomy after the 16th century reserved nervus for nerves; nervosus sinewy, turning nervous in the 19th century, e-nervare to take the sinews out — to enervate, nerve went from sinew (14th century) to nerve (17th) to courage (19th), nervy, neurosis 1777, neuron 1884."],
  pauc: ["paucus(少)来自印欧语 *peh₂u-(少、小),同根有 pauper(穷——\"生产得少的\")、puer(男孩)、pullus(幼崽)、英语 few、foal、希腊语 pais(孩子)。罗马人 pauci 是少数人;paucitas 稀少(paucity),pauper 穷人(pauper 英语 1510 年代;poor 经法语 povre),paupertas 贫穷(poverty),de-pauperare 使贫(depauperate),pauperize,poco 意大利语\"少\"(poco a poco),peu 法语,poco 西班牙语。",
    "paucus (few) comes from PIE *peh₂u- (few, small), shared with pauper (poor — \"producing little\"), puer (boy), pullus (a young animal), English few and foal, Greek pais (child). Roman pauci are the few; paucitas fewness (paucity), pauper a poor man (pauper in English from the 1510s; poor through French povre), paupertas poverty, de-pauperare to impoverish (depauperate), pauperize, Italian poco \"little\" (poco a poco), French peu, Spanish poco."],
  plumb: ["plumbum(铅)与希腊语 molybdos(铅——钼 molybdenum)可能同出一个地中海古语借词。罗马人用铅做水管(plumbarius 铅管工——plumber 直到 19 世纪还是铅工)、做测深锤;plumbum 挂在绳上就是 plumb line 铅垂线——plumb 垂直,plummet 铅锤,plunge 是 plumbicare(像铅一样沉下去)经法语 plongier,aplomb 是法语 à plomb\"按铅垂线\"——稳当,plomb 法语的铅,plombier 水管工,piombo 意大利语,plomo 西班牙语。",
    "plumbum (lead) and Greek molybdos (lead — molybdenum) probably descend from one old Mediterranean loan. Romans made water-pipes of lead (a plumbarius was a lead-worker — a plumber was still that until the 19th century) and sounding-weights; lead on a string is the plumb line — plumb means vertical, a plummet the lead weight, plunge is plumbicare, to sink like lead, through French plongier, aplomb French à plomb, \"true to the plumb-line\" — steadiness, French plomb lead, plombier a plumber, Italian piombo, Spanish plomo."],
  stud: ["studere(热心于、努力)来自印欧语 *(s)teu-(推、打)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 speudein(急切)和 spoudē(热忱)。罗马人的 studium 首先是热情、偏爱(studia 是党派热情),然后是求知的热心——学习;studiosus 热心的(studious),student 中世纪拉丁语,studio 是意大利语\"书房\"——画家的工作室(1810 年代),étude 法语的练习曲,studium generale 中世纪的大学,study 经法语 estudie,estudiante 西班牙语,Studium 德语的学业。",
    "studere (to be eager, to strive) may come from PIE *(s)teu- (to push, strike), with Greek speudein (to hurry) and spoudē (zeal) as possible cousins. Roman studium was first eagerness, partisanship (studia, party passions), then the eagerness to learn — study; studiosus zealous (studious), student medieval Latin, studio Italian for a study — a painter's workroom (1810s), étude the French practice-piece, studium generale the medieval university, study through French estudie, Spanish estudiante, German Studium."],
  cyt: ["希腊语 kytos(容器、空腔)来自印欧语 *(s)keu-(盖),同根有 kyein(怀孕)、拉丁语 cutis(皮肤)、英语 hide(兽皮)、house。生物学在 1850 年代把 kytos 借来指细胞(cell 的希腊语对应):cyto-logy 细胞学(1857),leuko-cyte 白细胞(1870),erythro-cyte 红细胞,lympho-cyte 淋巴细胞(1890),cyto-plasm 细胞质(1874),phago-cyte 吞噬细胞(梅契尼科夫 1884),cyto-kine 细胞因子(1974),cytotoxic 细胞毒的。",
    "Greek kytos (a vessel, a hollow) comes from PIE *(s)keu- (to cover), shared with kyein (to be pregnant), Latin cutis (skin), English hide and house. Biology borrowed kytos in the 1850s as the Greek for cell: cyto-logy (1857), leuko-cyte white cell (1870), erythro-cyte red cell, lympho-cyte (1890), cyto-plasm (1874), phago-cyte the eating cell (Metchnikoff 1884), cyto-kine (1974), cytotoxic."],
  drom: ["希腊语 dromos(跑、跑道)来自 dramein(跑),印欧语 *drem-(跑),同根有梵语 drámati(跑)。斯巴达的 dromos 是跑道;hippo-dromos 赛马场,syn-dromē 是\"一起跑来的\"——一组同时出现的症状(syndrome,1540 年代医学),dromedarius 是\"跑的骆驼\"(dromedary,单峰骆驼跑得快),palin-dromos 往回跑(palindrome,1620 年代),aero-drome 1900 年代,velo-drome 1890 年代,pro-dromos 先跑的——前驱症状,cata-dromous 降河洄游(鳗鱼)、ana-dromous 溯河洄游(鲑鱼)。",
    "Greek dromos (a running, a racecourse) comes from dramein (to run), PIE *drem- (to run), shared with Sanskrit drámati (runs). Sparta's dromos was its running-track; a hippo-dromos a horse-course, a syn-dromē \"a running together\" — a set of symptoms that appear at once (syndrome, medical since the 1540s), a dromedarius the running camel (dromedary), a palin-dromos running back (palindrome, 1620s), aero-drome 1900s, velo-drome 1890s, pro-dromos running before — a prodrome, cata-dromous running down to the sea (eels), ana-dromous running up-river (salmon)."],
  aesth: ["希腊语 aisthanesthai(感知)来自印欧语 *h₂ew-is-(感知),与拉丁语 audire(听)同根。aisthēsis 是感官知觉;aisthētikos 感官的——鲍姆加滕 1735 年用 aesthetica 命名\"感性认识的科学\",康德之后专指美学(aesthetics),an-aisthēsia 无感觉——1846 年霍姆斯提议用 anaesthesia 命名乙醚麻醉,syn-aisthēsia 联觉,aesthete 唯美主义者(1880 年代,王尔德时代),kin-aesthesia 动觉,anaesthetist 麻醉师。",
    "Greek aisthanesthai (to perceive) comes from PIE *h₂ew-is- (to perceive), kin to Latin audire (to hear). aisthēsis is sense-perception; aisthētikos of the senses — Baumgarten in 1735 named aesthetica the science of sensory knowledge, and after Kant it meant the philosophy of beauty (aesthetics), an-aisthēsia absence of feeling — in 1846 Oliver Wendell Holmes proposed anaesthesia for ether narcosis, syn-aisthēsia joined sensation, aesthete the devotee of beauty (1880s, Wilde's day), kin-aesthesia the sense of movement, anaesthetist."],
  urb: ["urbs(城)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——有人联系伊特鲁里亚语,有人联系 orbis(圆——城墙划的圈)或 verbena(圣枝)。对罗马人 Urbs 就是罗马本城(urbi et orbi——向城与世界);urbanus 城里的——文雅(urbane 是 17 世纪的\"有教养\",urban 1620 年代指城市的),urbanitas 都市风度,sub-urbium 城下的地带(suburb),con-urbation 1915 年格迪斯造,urbanize 1880 年代,Urbino、Orvieto(urbs vetus 老城)。",
    "urbs (a city) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — linked by some to Etruscan, by others to orbis (a circle — the ring of the walls) or verbena (the sacred bough). To Romans the Urbs was Rome itself (urbi et orbi — to the city and the world); urbanus of the city — polished (urbane in the 17th century meant cultivated, urban from the 1620s meant of the town), urbanitas city manners, sub-urbium the district below the city (suburb), conurbation Geddes' coinage of 1915, urbanize 1880s, Urbino, Orvieto (urbs vetus, old town)."],
  phon: ["希腊语 phōnē(声音、说话)来自印欧语 *bʰeh₂-(说),与拉丁语 fari、fama 同根。sym-phōnia 是\"一起发声\"——和谐(symphony 在中世纪指一种乐器,交响曲义 18 世纪),tele-phōnē 远声(贝尔 1876 年前已有此词指传声装置),phōnē-ma 音位(1895),phono-graph 记声机(爱迪生 1877),micro-phōnē 1683 年造指助听器,mega-phone 1878,eu-phōnia 好声(euphony),caco-phōnia 恶声,phon-etic 1826,saxo-phone 是萨克斯 1846 年的\"萨克斯之声\",xylophone 木声,homo-phone 同音异形。",
    "Greek phōnē (voice, sound) comes from PIE *bʰeh₂- (to speak), kin to Latin fari and fama. sym-phōnia is sounding together — concord (a symphony was a medieval instrument before the 18th-century orchestral form), tele-phōnē far-voice (used of speaking-tubes before Bell's 1876 device), phōnē-ma a phoneme (1895), phono-graph Edison's 1877 sound-writer, micro-phōnē coined in 1683 for an ear-trumpet, mega-phone 1878, eu-phōnia good sound (euphony), caco-phōnia bad sound, phon-etic 1826, saxo-phone Sax's voice of 1846, xylophone wood-voice, homo-phone same-sound."],
  opt: ["optare(选择、愿望)来自印欧语 *h₁op-(选)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 无、奥斯坎语 ufteis。罗马人 optare 是挑选、许愿;optio 是百夫长挑选的副手(option 选择权 1600 年代),optimus(最好的)可能同根——\"被选出的\"(optimism 是伏尔泰嘲讽莱布尼茨\"最好的世界\"时流行的词,1737),ad-optare 选来当儿子(adopt),co-optare 选入(co-opt),optative 祈愿语气,opt 1870 年代从法语 opter,optimize 1844,Optimus 是朱庇特的称号 Iuppiter Optimus Maximus(最好最大)。",
    "optare (to choose, wish) may come from PIE *h₁op- (to choose); Oscan ufteis is a possible cousin. Romans optare to pick, to wish for; an optio was the deputy a centurion chose (option, 1600s), optimus (best) is probably kin — \"the chosen\" (optimism spread when Voltaire mocked Leibniz's best of all worlds, 1737), ad-optare to choose as a son (adopt), co-optare to choose into a body (co-opt), the optative mood of wishing, opt from French opter (1870s), optimize 1844, Optimus the title of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, best and greatest."],
  clin: ["clinare(倾斜)来自印欧语 *ḱlei-(靠、斜),同根有希腊语 klinein(倾斜)、klinē(床——躺的地方)、英语 lean、ladder(靠的梯子)、德语 lehnen、Leiter。罗马人 de-clinare 是偏离——语法上的\"变格\"(词形从主格\"偏离\"——decline;衰落义 15 世纪),in-clinare 倾向(incline、inclination),re-clinare 靠回(recline),clivus 斜坡(pro-clivis 顺坡的——倾向,proclivity),climax 是希腊语 klimax\"梯子\"——修辞的递升,climate 是 klima\"天空的倾斜\"——纬度带(古人以太阳角度分气候带),clinic 是 klinikos\"床边的\"——医生床边教学(clinic 1620 年代),clinical 床边的,client 是 cliens——\"靠着\"保护人的门客,-cline 单斜、向斜(地质),clitoris 与此无关。",
    "clinare (to lean) comes from PIE *ḱlei- (to lean), shared with Greek klinein (to slope), klinē (a bed — where one lies), English lean and ladder (what leans), German lehnen, Leiter. Roman de-clinare is to turn aside — in grammar to decline a noun (its forms \"lean away\" from the nominative; the sense of decay is 15th-century), in-clinare to lean toward (incline, inclination), re-clinare to lean back (recline), clivus a slope (pro-clivis downhill — a proclivity), climax is Greek klimax, a ladder — the rhetorical ascent, climate is klima, the sky's inclination — a latitude band (ancients divided the earth by the sun's angle), clinic is klinikos, at the bedside — bedside teaching (1620s), clinical, a client is a cliens, one who leans on a patron, -cline in geology; clitoris is unrelated."],
});
